var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/live_robot/#Live-robot-data","page":"Live robot data","text":"A diagnostic view of a running robot needs two things: things that move (the robot body, sensor frames) and data that keeps arriving (LiDAR scans). SpatialRender handles the first with poses and the second with streaming point clouds. Both can be updated from any task or thread while the viewer is open.","title":"Live robot data"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/live_robot/#A-moving-body","page":"Live robot data","text":"Every layer has a pose: a transform from its own coordinates into the scene. set_pose! changes it without touching the vertex data, so moving a mesh is as cheap as setting one matrix.\n\nusing SpatialRender, Random\nscene = Scene()\nbody = [Vec3f(x, y, z) for z in (0, 0.6) for y in (-0.5, 0.5) for x in (-0.9, 0.9)]\nfaces = [(1, 2, 4), (1, 4, 3), (5, 7, 8), (5, 8, 6), (1, 5, 6), (1, 6, 2),\n         (3, 4, 8), (3, 8, 7), (1, 3, 7), (1, 7, 5), (2, 6, 8), (2, 8, 4)]\nverts = [MeshVertex(body[k], Vec3f(0, 0, 1)) for f in faces for k in f]\nrover = TriMesh(verts, UInt32.(0:length(verts)-1); name = \"rover\", color = Vec4f(0.95, 0.55, 0.15, 1))\ncompute_normals!(rover)\nadd!(scene, rover)\nset_pose!(rover, Vec3d(4, 2, 0), Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), π / 4))   # 4 m east, 2 m north, facing NE","title":"A moving body"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/live_robot/#Streaming-scans","page":"Live robot data","text":"A StreamingPointCloud is a ring buffer: it holds the most recent capacity points, and only newly arrived points are sent to the GPU. push_scan! takes points in the sensor's frame plus the sensor's pose at the moment of the scan, so every scan lands where it was taken even while the robot moves.\n\nHere a simulated rotating LiDAR scans a field of posts while the rover drives a circle:\n\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nposts = [(8cos(a), 8sin(a)) for a in range(0, 2π, length = 13)[1:12]]\nfunction scan(position, yaw)          # returns points in the sensor frame\n    pts = Vec3f[]\n    for az in range(0, 2π, length = 721)[1:720], el in range(-0.3, 0.05, length = 12)\n        d = Vec3d(cos(az) * cos(el), sin(az) * cos(el), sin(el))\n        dw = Vec3d(cos(yaw) * d[1] - sin(yaw) * d[2], sin(yaw) * d[1] + cos(yaw) * d[2], d[3])\n        r = d[3] < 0 ? -position[3] / dw[3] : Inf            # ground hit\n        for (px, py) in posts                                 # post hits (radius 0.3 m)\n            ox, oy = position[1] - px, position[2] - py\n            b = ox * dw[1] + oy * dw[2]; c = ox^2 + oy^2 - 0.09\n            disc = b^2 - c * (dw[1]^2 + dw[2]^2)\n            disc > 0 && (t = (-b - sqrt(disc)) / (dw[1]^2 + dw[2]^2); 0 < t < r && (r = t))\n        end\n        r < 25 && push!(pts, Vec3f((r + 0.01randn(rng)) * d))\n    end\n    return pts\nend\n\nlidar = add!(scene, StreamingPointCloud(500_000; name = \"lidar\", max_age = 40))\ntrail = Vec3d[]\nfor t in 0:0.25:12\n    position = Vec3d(4cos(t / 2), 4sin(t / 2), 0); yaw = t / 2 + π / 2\n    set_pose!(rover, position, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw))\n    sensor = position + Vec3d(0, 0, 1.2)\n    push_scan!(lidar, scan(sensor, yaw); pose = (sensor, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw)))\n    push!(trail, position + Vec3d(0, 0, 0.05))\nend\nadd!(scene, trajectory(trail; width = 3, name = \"trail\"))\nprintln(length(lidar), \" points from \", lidar.seq, \" scans\")\n303908 points from 49 scans\n\nThe cloud uses COLOR_AGE by default: the newest scan is bright, older ones fade, so you can see what the robot sees now against what it saw earlier.\n\ncamera = Camera(position = Vec3d(-12, -16, 13), target = Vec3d(0, 0, 0.5))\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe rover and its scans, coloured by age","title":"Streaming scans"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/live_robot/#From-another-task-or-thread","page":"Live robot data","text":"In a real application the scans come from a driver or a ROS node, not a loop. Updates are thread-safe; open the viewer with block = false, feed it from a task, and run the window loop:\n\nv = open_viewer(scene; block = false)\nfeeder = Threads.@spawn while v.open\n    position, yaw, points = next_message()           # your sensor interface\n    set_pose!(rover, position, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw))\n    push_scan!(lidar, points; pose = (position, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw)))\nend\nrun!(v)\n\nexamples/live.jl in the repository runs exactly this with a simulated 16-beam LiDAR at 10 Hz.","title":"From another task or thread"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/live_robot/#Measuring","page":"Live robot data","text":"Annotations hold text labels and measurements, drawn on top of everything. measure! adds a distance with its height difference and slope; the viewer's measure mode (R, then click two points) calls it for you.\n\nnotes = add!(scene, Annotations())\nmeasure!(notes, Vec3f(posts[1]..., 0), Vec3f(posts[4]..., 0))\nannotate!(notes, Vec3f(4cos(6), 4sin(6), 1.5), \"rover\")\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nA measurement between two posts and a label\n\nNext: Replaying ROS 2 bags.","title":"Measuring"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#SpatialRender.SpatialRender","page":"API","text":"SpatialRender\n\nA native OpenGL renderer for meshes, LiDAR point clouds, GIS vector layers and trajectories in one scene, built for diagnostic views in robotics and aerial systems.\n\nThe package has two halves:\n\na GPU-free core (ingestion, local frames, filters, BVH / k-d tree indexing, frustum culling, ray picking, camera) that can be used and tested headless;\nan OpenGL 4.1 core-profile backend (GLFW window, interleaved vertex buffers, point / mesh / line shader pipelines, render loop).\n\nQuick start:\n\nusing SpatialRender\nscene = Scene()\nadd!(scene, load_mesh(\"part.stl\"))\nload!(scene, \"scan.las\")   # sets the scene frame from the file\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.SpatialRender"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.AABB","page":"API","text":"AABB(lo, hi)\n\nAxis-aligned bounding box. AABB() is the empty box (lo = +Inf, hi = -Inf), the identity of union.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.AABB"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Annotations","page":"API","text":"Annotations(; name=\"annotations\", width=2)\n\nA layer of labels and measurement lines, drawn on top of the scene (never hidden by geometry). Add to it with annotate! and measure!; undo! removes the last addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Annotations"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.AttributeOutlierFilter","page":"API","text":"AttributeOutlierFilter(; attributes=(:z, :intensity), method=\"mad\", threshold=nothing, window=7)\n\nOne-dimensional outlier rejection on per-point attributes (:x, :y, :z, :intensity); a point is dropped when any listed attribute is an outlier. Methods: \"zscore\", \"iqr\", \"mad\" (default, robust) and \"hampel\" (a moving-window MAD along acquisition order, GPS time when present, which follows a drifting signal: intensity spikes along a scan line).\n\nThe statistics are computed by the KaosCleaner engine: using KaosCleaner enables this filter (package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.AttributeOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.BVH","page":"API","text":"BVH\n\nBounding volume hierarchy over one TriMesh, for ray casting (picking, measurements, line-of-sight checks) and spatial queries. Build with build_bvh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.BVH"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.BagPlayer","page":"API","text":"BagPlayer\n\nReplays an MCAP / rosbag2 file into a Scene. Create with open_bag; drive with play!, pause!, seek!, tick! — or hand it to open_viewer(scene; player), which ticks it every frame and maps Space / ← → / [ ] / Home.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.BagPlayer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Camera","page":"API","text":"Camera(; position, target, fov=50°, near=0.05, far=1e4)\n\nPerspective (or orthographic) camera with a quaternion orientation. distance is the distance to the pivot used by orbit, pan and zoom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Camera"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.CameraController","page":"API","text":"CameraController(camera; mode=OrbitMode)\n\nMaps input to camera motion.\n\ninput orbit mode fly mode\nleft drag orbit about pivot look around\nright / middle / shift-left pan pan\nscroll zoom to pivot change fly speed\nW A S D, Q E — move, down / up\nshift (held) — move 4× faster\nTab switch to fly switch to orbit\nF frame scene frame scene\ndouble-click pivot on picked point pivot on picked point\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.CameraController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.CropBox","page":"API","text":"CropBox(lo, hi; invert=false)\n\nKeep points inside (or with invert=true, outside) an axis-aligned box in the local frame, e.g. to cut the ego-vehicle out of a scan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.CropBox"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.DrawRange","page":"API","text":"DrawRange(first, count)\n\nOne glMultiDrawArrays range (documentation type: VisibleRanges stores ranges as two parallel Int32 arrays, exactly what OpenGL reads).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.DrawRange"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.ENUFrame","page":"API","text":"ENUFrame(lat0, lon0, h0=0)\n\nLocal East-North-Up tangent frame at a geodetic origin (degrees, metres). Source points are (lon, lat[, h]), the GeoJSON axis order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ENUFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.FilterPipeline","page":"API","text":"FilterPipeline(filters...)\n\nRun filters in order; each sees the output of the previous one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.FilterPipeline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.FrameTree","page":"API","text":"FrameTree(; max_gap=2.0)\n\nFrames and their transforms over time, filled from /tf and /tf_static (see add_transform!) and queried with lookup_transform. Between two samples more than max_gap seconds apart (a recording gap), the nearest sample is used instead of interpolating motion that wasn't recorded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.FrameTree"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Frustum","page":"API","text":"Frustum\n\nSix planes (n, d) with n ⋅ p + d >= 0 inside, extracted from a view-projection matrix (Gribb–Hartmann).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Frustum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.GroundPlaneFilter","page":"API","text":"GroundPlaneFilter(; threshold=0.15, iterations=200, max_tilt=15, mode=:remove, seed=1)\n\nFit the dominant near-horizontal plane with RANSAC (normal within max_tilt degrees of +Z) and treat points within threshold metres of it as ground.\n\nmode=:remove drops ground points (obstacle / structure views);\nmode=:keep keeps only ground points;\nmode=:classify keeps everything and sets ASPRS class 2 (ground) on the inliers, so the classification color mode shows them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.GroundPlaneFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.IdentityFrame","page":"API","text":"IdentityFrame()\n\nCoordinates are already local (CAD parts, simulated data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.IdentityFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.InputState","page":"API","text":"Keyboard / mouse state shared between callbacks and the frame update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.InputState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.LASHeader","page":"API","text":"Fields of a LAS public header block that the reader needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.LASHeader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Label","page":"API","text":"Label(position, text; color=white, size=13, marker=true)\n\nScreen-aligned text anchored at a 3-D position; size is the cap height in pixels. marker draws a small × at the anchor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Label"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.LineLayer","page":"API","text":"LineLayer(polylines; name, width)\n\nPolylines (GIS boundaries, roads, trajectories) drawn as screen-space, anti-aliased ribbons width pixels wide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.LineLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.LineVertex","page":"API","text":"Per-segment instance record for screen-space lines: endpoints and an RGBA color (40 bytes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.LineVertex"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.LocalFrame","page":"API","text":"A mapping from source coordinates (Float64) to the scene's local Cartesian frame (metres, Z up).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.LocalFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.MCAPFile","page":"API","text":"MCAPFile\n\nAn opened MCAP recording: schemas, channels and a time-sorted index of every message. A split rosbag2 recording (several .mcap files) is one MCAPFile with one timeline. Payloads are read lazily (message_data); compressed chunks are decompressed on demand and a few are cached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.MCAPFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.MCAPWriter","page":"API","text":"MCAPWriter(path; profile=\"ros2\", compression=\"zstd\", chunk_size=1 << 20)\n\nWrite an MCAP file: add_schema!, add_channel!, write_message!, then close. Messages are grouped into chunks compressed with compression (\"zstd\", \"lz4\" or \"\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.MCAPWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.MeshVertex","page":"API","text":"32-byte interleaved mesh vertex: position, normal, uv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.MeshVertex"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.OctreeCloud","page":"API","text":"OctreeCloud(dir; point_budget=3_000_000, min_node_pixels=80, point_size=2, color_mode)\n\nA point cloud converted with build_octree and streamed from disk. With adaptive_size (default) each node's points are drawn at a world size matching that node's point spacing (never below point_size pixels), so coarse regions read as continuous surfaces rather than sparse dots.\n\nStreaming details: only the nodes that matter for the current view are loaded (on a background thread) and kept on the GPU, within point_budget points. Nodes are refined while their projected size exceeds min_node_pixels.\n\nThe layer's coordinates are relative to frame(cloud) (an OffsetFrame at the data centre); load other data with that frame to overlay it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.OctreeCloud"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.OffsetFrame","page":"API","text":"OffsetFrame(origin)\n\nFor projected coordinates (UTM, state plane, LAS files): subtract origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.OffsetFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.PointCloud","page":"API","text":"PointCloud(vertices; name=\"points\", point_size=2, color_mode=COLOR_HEIGHT)\n\nA point cloud in the scene's local frame. vertices is the interleaved Vector{PointVertex} that goes to the GPU as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.PointCloud"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.PointCloud-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"Point cloud from a 3×N (or N×3) matrix or a vector of points, with optional attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.PointCloud"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.PointFilter","page":"API","text":"Supertype of point cloud filters. Implement keep_mask(f, pc)::BitVector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.PointFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.PointIndex","page":"API","text":"PointIndex\n\nChunked k-d tree over a PointCloud, used for culling, level of detail, picking and neighbour queries. Build with build_point_index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.PointIndex"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.PointVertex","page":"API","text":"PointVertex(position, intensity=0, rgba=white, classification=0)\n\n20-byte interleaved point record (no padding; every GPU attribute offset is a multiple of 4, which D3D12-backed drivers need):\n\noffset field type\n0 position 3 × Float32\n12 rgba 4 × UInt8\n16 intensity UInt16 (0–65535, as in LAS)\n18 classification UInt8 (ASPRS)\n19 flags UInt8 (streaming: scan number)\n\nThe convenience constructor takes intensity as a 0–1 float; read it back as 0–1 with intensity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.PointVertex"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Polyline","page":"API","text":"A polyline in the local frame with one color.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Polyline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Quat","page":"API","text":"Quat(w, x, y, z)\n\nRotation quaternion w + xi + yj + zk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Quat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Quat-Tuple{AbstractVector, Real}","page":"API","text":"Rotation of θ radians about axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Quat"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Quat-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3}}","page":"API","text":"Quaternion from an orthonormal rotation matrix (Shepperd's method).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Quat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.RadiusOutlierFilter","page":"API","text":"RadiusOutlierFilter(; radius, min_neighbors=4)\n\nDrop points with fewer than min_neighbors other points within radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.RadiusOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Ray","page":"API","text":"Ray(origin, direction)\n\nA ray in the local frame. direction is normalised; inv caches its reciprocal for slab tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Ray"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.RayHit","page":"API","text":"RayHit\n\nResult of a pick: distance t along the ray, hit position, the layer hit, and the 1-based index of the triangle or point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.RayHit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.RenderSettings","page":"API","text":"RenderSettings(; kwargs...)\n\nbackground: clear color.\npoint_size_mode: :pixels (constant screen size) or :world (metres, shrinks with distance; world_point_size).\nround_points: circular splats with sphere shading (false: squares).\nlod_density: points drawn per pixel² of a chunk's screen footprint; Inf disables level of detail.\nwireframe_color: overlay color for meshes with wireframe = true.\ngrid: draw a ground grid at z = 0.\nedl, edl_strength, edl_radius: eye-dome lighting on points and meshes (screen-space depth shading; radius in pixels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.RenderSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Renderer","page":"API","text":"Renderer(scene; settings=RenderSettings())\n\nOwns the GL programs and per-layer GPU objects for one scene in the current GL context. Layers are uploaded lazily whenever scene.version changes; removed layers are freed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Renderer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Rigid","page":"API","text":"Rigid transform p ↦ R p + t (Float64; composed on the CPU, converted for the GPU).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Rigid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Scene","page":"API","text":"Scene(; frame=IdentityFrame())\n\nA collection of layers sharing one local frame. Loaders accept frame= so data from different sources lands in the same coordinates; scene.frame is the natural value to pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Scene"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.ShaderSource","page":"API","text":"ShaderSource(name, vertex, fragment)\n\nA named vertex + fragment shader pair. Programs are compiled once per GL context and cached by a hash of their source (see compile_cache_stats); validate_shaders checks every built-in pair offline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ShaderSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Snapshot","page":"API","text":"Snapshot\n\nA rendered image that displays itself: inline in Jupyter, VS Code and Pluto, and as a picture in Documenter pages. Create with snapshot; the pixels are in .pixels (rows top to bottom), write_png(path, s.pixels) saves them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Snapshot"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.StampedTransform","page":"API","text":"A stamped rigid transform (tf2_msgs/TFMessage entry, or a pose).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.StampedTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.StatisticalOutlierFilter","page":"API","text":"StatisticalOutlierFilter(; k=8, nsigma=2.0)\n\nStatistical outlier removal (as in PCL's StatisticalOutlierRemoval): for each point, the mean distance to its k nearest neighbours; points whose mean distance exceeds the global mean by more than nsigma standard deviations are dropped. Removes isolated returns (dust, rain, multipath).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.StatisticalOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.StreamingPointCloud","page":"API","text":"StreamingPointCloud(capacity; name=\"live\", point_size=2, color_mode=COLOR_AGE, max_age=50)\n\nA point layer for live sensor feeds holding the most recent capacity points. Fill it with push_scan!. COLOR_AGE shades points by how many scans ago they arrived (newest bright, max_age scans and older dark).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.StreamingPointCloud"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.TriMesh","page":"API","text":"TriMesh(vertices, indices; name, color, wireframe)\n\nIndexed triangle mesh. indices holds 0-based UInt32 vertex indices, three per triangle, exactly as glDrawElements reads them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.TriMesh"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.Viewer","page":"API","text":"Viewer\n\nAn open window: the GLFW handle, the Renderer and the CameraController. Returned by open_viewer when block=false so the scene can be changed while the window is open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Viewer"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.VisibleRanges","page":"API","text":"VisibleRanges(capacity)\n\nPre-allocated output of cull!, reused every frame (no allocation in the render loop).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.VisibleRanges"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SpatialRender.VoxelDownsample","page":"API","text":"VoxelDownsample(voxel)\n\nKeep one point per voxel-sized cube (the point nearest the voxel centre), so attributes stay real sensor values rather than averages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.VoxelDownsample"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.add!-Tuple{Scene, SpatialRender.Layer}","page":"API","text":"add!(scene, layer) -> layer\n\nAdd a layer to the scene. Returns the layer so it can be tweaked afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.add!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.add_channel!-Tuple{MCAPWriter, AbstractString, Integer}","page":"API","text":"Register a channel on topic using schema schema_id; returns its id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.add_channel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.add_schema!","page":"API","text":"Register a schema (e.g. \"sensor_msgs/msg/PointCloud2\", encoding \"ros2msg\"); returns its id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.add_schema!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.add_transform!-Tuple{FrameTree, StampedTransform}","page":"API","text":"add_transform!(tree, t::StampedTransform; static=false)\n\nRecord parent ← child at t.stamp. Samples may arrive in any order; call SpatialRender.finalize! once after loading to sort them. A frame that changes parent keeps the first one (tf2 forbids reparenting) and a warning is shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.add_transform!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.annotate!-Tuple{Annotations, Any, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"annotate!(ann, position, text; color, size=13, marker=true) -> ann\n\nAdd a text label at position (layer frame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.annotate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.apply_filter-Tuple{PointFilter, PointCloud}","page":"API","text":"apply_filter(f, pc) -> PointCloud\n\nA new cloud holding the points f keeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.apply_filter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.bounds-Tuple{Scene}","page":"API","text":"Bounds of all visible layers in the scene frame (poses applied).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.build_bvh-Tuple{TriMesh}","page":"API","text":"build_bvh(mesh; leaf_size=4) -> BVH\n\nTop-down build with a 12-bin surface-area heuristic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.build_bvh"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.build_octree-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"build_octree(source, out_dir; node_capacity=50_000, bucket_points=4_000_000,\n             spacing_div=128, chunk=1_000_000, seed=1) -> out_dir\n\nConvert a point cloud for out-of-core rendering with OctreeCloud. source is a LAS file path or a PointCloud (whose coordinates are taken as already local).\n\nnode_capacity: maximum points stored in one node.\nbucket_points: target points per on-disk bucket. Peak memory is about bucket_points × 24 bytes plus write buffers.\nspacing_div: subsampling grid per node edge; larger keeps more points in coarse nodes.\n\nThe LAS path is read in chunk-point slices through a memory map, so files larger than RAM convert. Coordinates are stored relative to the cloud's centre (an OffsetFrame recorded in octree.json).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.build_octree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.build_point_index-Tuple{PointCloud}","page":"API","text":"build_point_index(pc; chunk_size=8192, seed=1) -> PointIndex\n\nReorders pc.vertices (and pc.gps_time) in place into spatially coherent chunks of at most chunk_size points (median splits on the longest axis) and shuffles points within each chunk. Extra memory while building is one Int32 permutation (4 bytes per point); what stays is one entry per chunk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.build_point_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.compile_cache_stats-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"compile_cache_stats() -> (hits, misses)\n\nHow often a shader program was served from the cache versus compiled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.compile_cache_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.compute_normals!-Tuple{TriMesh}","page":"API","text":"Compute smooth per-vertex normals (area weighted) in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.compute_normals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.configure_gpu!-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"configure_gpu!(; candidates=nothing, force=false) -> String\n\nMake OpenGL use a hardware GPU on WSL2 (see the comment at the top of src/render/gpu_select.jl). Called automatically before the first window; returns a description of what was selected.\n\nDoes nothing when not on WSL, when GALLIUM_DRIVER is already set (your choice wins), or when SPATIALRENDER_GPU_AUTOCONFIG=0. candidates are adapter-name substrings to try in order (default: an explicitly set MESA_D3D12_DEFAULT_ADAPTER_NAME, else NVIDIA, AMD, Intel, then Mesa's default adapter).\n\nprobe (returns the renderer string of a fresh context, or nothing), reinit and wsl exist so tests can simulate drivers; leave them alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.configure_gpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.cull!-Tuple{PointIndex, Frustum}","page":"API","text":"cull!(index, frustum; eye, pixels_per_radian=Inf, density=1.0, min_points=256) -> VisibleRanges\n\nFill index.visible with the draw ranges of chunks inside frustum.\n\nLevel of detail: a chunk whose bounding sphere covers A pixels on screen (computed from the distance to eye and pixels_per_radian) draws at most density × A of its points, never fewer than min_points. Because chunks are shuffled, the drawn prefix is a uniform subsample. Pass pixels_per_radian=Inf to always draw every point.\n\nAdjacent full chunks are merged into one range. Allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.cull!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.current_time-Tuple{BagPlayer}","page":"API","text":"Current playback position in seconds from the start of the bag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.current_time"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_navsatfix-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"sensormsgs/NavSatFix → (stamp, frameid, status, lat, lon, alt). status < 0 means no fix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_navsatfix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_odometry-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"navmsgs/Odometry → StampedTransform (pose of childframeid in header.frameid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_odometry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_path-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"navmsgs/Path → (stamp, frameid, poses::Vector{Vec3d}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_pointcloud2-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"decode_pointcloud2(d; intensity_range=nothing) -> (stamp, frame_id, Vector{PointVertex})\n\nDecode a sensormsgs/PointCloud2. Uses the x, y, z fields (float32 or float64), intensity (any numeric type; normalised by `intensityrange, default 255 for floats and the type's maximum for integers),rgb/rgba(packed) andring` (stored as the classification, so class colouring shows LiDAR rings). Non-finite points are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_pointcloud2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_pose_stamped-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"geometry_msgs/PoseStamped → StampedTransform (child = \"\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_pose_stamped"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.decode_tf-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"geometrymsgs/TransformStamped[] from a tf2msgs/TFMessage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_tf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.dolly!-Tuple{Camera, Real}","page":"API","text":"dolly!(cam, steps; min_distance=1e-3)\n\nMove toward (steps > 0) or away from the pivot geometrically (10% per step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.dolly!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.ecef_to_geodetic-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"ecef_to_geodetic(p) -> (lat, lon, h)\n\nInverse of geodetic_to_ecef (Bowring's method with two refinement iterations; sub-millimetre near the Earth's surface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ecef_to_geodetic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_navsatfix-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"API","text":"Encode a sensor_msgs/NavSatFix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_navsatfix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_odometry-Tuple{StampedTransform}","page":"API","text":"Encode a nav_msgs/Odometry (zero covariance and twist).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_odometry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_path-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Encode a nav_msgs/Path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_pointcloud2-Tuple{Any, Any, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"Encode a sensor_msgs/PointCloud2 with float32 x, y, z, intensity and uint16 ring (Velodyne-like layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_pointcloud2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_pose_stamped-Tuple{StampedTransform}","page":"API","text":"Encode a geometry_msgs/PoseStamped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_pose_stamped"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.encode_tf-Tuple{AbstractVector{StampedTransform}}","page":"API","text":"Encode a tf2_msgs/TFMessage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.encode_tf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.fit_ground_plane-Tuple{PointCloud}","page":"API","text":"fit_ground_plane(pc; threshold=0.15, iterations=200, max_tilt=15, seed=1) -> (n, d)\n\nRANSAC plane n ⋅ p + d = 0 with unit normal n pointing up, refined by least squares on the inliers. Returns nothing when no plane is found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.fit_ground_plane"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.fly_look!-Tuple{Camera, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"fly_look!(cam, dyaw, dpitch)\n\nRotate the view in place (first-person mouse look), horizon kept level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.fly_look!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.fly_move!-Tuple{Camera, Any}","page":"API","text":"fly_move!(cam, v)\n\nMove by v in camera axes (v[1] right, v[2] up, v[3] forward), metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.fly_move!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.frame!-Tuple{Camera, AABB}","page":"API","text":"frame!(cam, box; margin=1.0)\n\nKeep the view direction and move so box fills the view; the pivot moves to the box centre. Also sets near / far planes for the box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.frame!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.frustum_from_matrix-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"API","text":"frustum_from_matrix(vp) -> Frustum\n\nPlanes of the clip volume of the OpenGL-convention view-projection matrix vp (clip z in [-w, w]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.frustum_from_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.geodetic_to_ecef","page":"API","text":"geodetic_to_ecef(lat, lon, h) -> Vec3d\n\nWGS84 latitude / longitude in degrees and ellipsoidal height in metres to Earth-centred, Earth-fixed coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.geodetic_to_ecef"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.gis_layer","page":"API","text":"gis_layer(geometries; frame=nothing, color, width=2, height=0, name=\"gis\") -> (LineLayer, PointCloud, frame)\n\nConvert GeoInterface-compatible geometries (a GeoDataFrames geometry column, Shapefile.jl / GeoJSON.jl / LibGEOS objects, …) into a line layer and a point-marker layer, like load_geojson.\n\nCoordinates go through frame: with an ENUFrame (or frame=nothing, which builds one at the data centre) they must be longitude/latitude, so reproject projected layers to EPSG:4326 first; with an OffsetFrame they are used as projected metres directly.\n\nusing GeoDataFrames\ndf = GeoDataFrames.read(\"roads.gpkg\")\nlines, marks, _ = gis_layer(df.geometry; frame = scene.frame)\nadd!(scene, lines)\n\nNeeds using GeoInterface (or any package that loads it, such as GeoDataFrames).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.gis_layer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.gl_info-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"gl_info() -> (vendor, renderer, version, software)\n\nDescribe the current GL context; software is true for CPU rasterisers (llvmpipe, softpipe, SwiftShader, Microsoft Basic Render).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.gl_info"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.ground_mask-Tuple{PointCloud}","page":"API","text":"ground_mask(pc; kwargs...) -> BitVector\n\nPoints within threshold of the RANSAC ground plane (see fit_ground_plane).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ground_mask"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.intensity-Tuple{PointVertex}","page":"API","text":"Intensity of a point as a Float32 in 0–1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.intersects-Tuple{Frustum, AABB}","page":"API","text":"intersects(frustum, box) -> Bool\n\nConservative test: false only when box is entirely outside one plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.intersects"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load!-Tuple{Scene, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load!(scene, path; kwargs...) -> scene\n\nLoad any supported file into scene in the scene's frame.\n\nWhen the scene is still empty and uses an IdentityFrame, georeferenced data sets the frame: a LAS file sets an OffsetFrame at its centre, a GeoJSON file an ENUFrame at its centre. Later files reuse that frame. A directory written by build_octree loads as an OctreeCloud.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_geojson-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_geojson(path; frame=nothing, color, width=2, height=0, name) -> (LineLayer, PointCloud, frame)\n\nRead a GeoJSON file. Lines and polygon rings become a LineLayer; Point/MultiPoint features become a PointCloud of markers.\n\nCoordinates are taken as WGS84 longitude/latitude and reprojected into frame. With frame=nothing an ENUFrame at the centre of the data's lon/lat bounds is used. height is added to 2-D coordinates (drape boundaries slightly above terrain). A feature property \"stroke\" holding a #rrggbb color overrides color.\n\nFor shapefiles, GeoPackage and other OGR formats load GeoDataFrames and use gis_layer (a package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_geojson"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_las-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_las(path; frame=nothing, name=basename(path), gps_time=false, stride=1,\n         classes=nothing) -> (PointCloud, frame)\n\nRead a LAS file through a memory map.\n\nframe: local frame for the points. When nothing, an OffsetFrame at the centre of the file's bounding box is used. The frame is returned too, so other layers can be loaded into the same coordinates.\nstride: keep every stride-th point (quick previews of huge files).\nclasses: keep only these ASPRS classes, e.g. classes=[2, 6].\ngps_time: also read per-point GPS time (formats 1, 3–10).\n\nIntensity is normalised to 0–1 by the largest value in the file; 16-bit RGB is reduced to 8 bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_las"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_laz-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_laz(path; kwargs...) -> (PointCloud, frame)\n\nDecompress a LAZ file with laszip or pdal (whichever is on PATH) into a temporary LAS file and read it with load_las.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_laz"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_mesh-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_mesh(path; kwargs...) -> TriMesh\n\nLoad a triangle mesh by extension: .stl and .obj natively. Other mesh formats (PLY, OFF, 3MF, …) load through FileIO/MeshIO once GeometryBasics is imported (package extension).\n\nSTEP / IGES are B-rep CAD formats, not meshes: tessellate them first (for example FreeCAD or OpenCASCADE STEP → STL export with a chosen tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_obj-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_obj(path; frame=IdentityFrame(), scale=1, name=basename(path)) -> TriMesh\n\nRead a Wavefront OBJ. Polygons are fan-triangulated; each distinct v/vt/vn combination becomes one GPU vertex. Missing normals are computed. OBJ files from modelling tools are usually Y-up: pass up=:y to convert them to the scene's Z-up convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_obj"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.load_stl-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_stl(path; smooth=false, frame=IdentityFrame(), name=basename(path), scale=1) -> TriMesh\n\nRead a binary or ASCII STL file.\n\nWith smooth=false (the default, right for CAD parts) every facet keeps its own three vertices and the facet normal, so hard edges stay hard. With smooth=true coincident vertices are welded and normals averaged. scale converts units (e.g. scale=1e-3 for millimetre CAD into metres). up=:y converts a Y-up model (common from 3D modelling tools) to the scene's Z-up convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_stl"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.look_at!-Tuple{Camera, Any}","page":"API","text":"look_at!(cam, target; up=Z) -> cam\n\nPoint the camera at target, keeping the horizon level (world Z up).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.look_at!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.lookup_transform-Tuple{FrameTree, AbstractString, AbstractString, Integer}","page":"API","text":"lookup_transform(tree, target, source, t) -> Union{Rigid, Nothing}\n\nTransform taking points in frame source to frame target at time t (nanoseconds), or nothing when the frames are not connected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.lookup_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.measure!-Tuple{Annotations, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"measure!(ann, p, q; color=yellow) -> (distance, dz, slope_degrees)\n\nAdd a measurement from p to q: the segment, its 3-D length, the height difference and the slope, labelled at the midpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.measure!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.measure_click!-Tuple{Viewer, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"measure_click!(viewer, x, y) -> Union{Nothing, Tuple}\n\nMeasure-mode click at framebuffer pixel (x, y): the first click marks a point, the second adds the measurement and returns (distance, dz, slope).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.measure_click!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.message_data-Tuple{MCAPFile, SpatialRender.MessageRef}","page":"API","text":"message_data(f, ref) -> AbstractVector{UInt8}\n\nPayload bytes of one indexed message (a view; copy it to keep it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.message_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.nearest_points-Tuple{PointIndex, Any, Integer}","page":"API","text":"nearest_points(index, p, k) -> (indices, distances)\n\nThe k nearest points to p, nearest first (1-based indices into the reordered cloud; Euclidean distances). Chunks are scanned in order of box distance and the search stops once no remaining chunk can contain a closer point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.nearest_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.open_bag-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"open_bag(path; scene=Scene(), fixed_frame=nothing, topics=nothing, cloud_capacity=2_000_000,\n         history=3.0, intensity_range=nothing, marker_size=nothing) -> BagPlayer\n\nOpen a ROS 2 bag (an .mcap file or a rosbag2 directory) for replay into scene and add a layer per supported topic:\n\nmessage type shown as\nsensor_msgs/PointCloud2 streaming cloud, each scan placed via TF at its stamp\nnav_msgs/Odometry, PoseStamped full trajectory + an axes marker at the current pose\nnav_msgs/Path the latest path at the current time\nsensor_msgs/NavSatFix ENU track, first fix anchored at the robot's pose\n\n/tf and /tf_static build the frame tree. Everything is drawn in fixed_frame (default: map, world, odom, else the tree's root). history seconds of scans stay visible (:auto: enough for the sparsest point cloud topic, 3–30 s). Sensor frames missing from TF (typically when /tf_static was not recorded) are drawn at the robot frame with missing_frames = :robot (or untransformed with :origin, or not at all with :skip); give the real mountings with static_transforms = [StampedTransform(...)]. TF is interpolated only across gaps up to max_tf_gap seconds. With skip_gaps, playback jumps over stretches with no messages. topics restricts which topics are shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_bag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.open_mcap-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"open_mcap(path) -> MCAPFile\n\nOpen an .mcap file, or a rosbag2 directory. A directory may hold a split recording (several .mcap files, as ros2 bag record --max-bag-size writes); all of them are read, in the order metadata.yaml lists them (else by name), into one time-sorted index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_mcap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.open_viewer-Tuple{Scene}","page":"API","text":"open_viewer(scene; width=1280, height=800, title, block=true, camera=nothing, settings,\n            vsync=true, player=nothing, visible=true) -> Viewer\n\nOpen a native window and render scene interactively (see HELP for controls; press H in the window). visible=false keeps the window hidden (automated tests, scripted captures). With block=false the function returns the Viewer immediately; call run!(viewer) or step!(viewer) to drive it, e.g. from a robot's own update loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_viewer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.orbit!-Tuple{Camera, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"orbit!(cam, dyaw, dpitch)\n\nTurntable orbit about the pivot: yaw about world Z, pitch about the camera's right axis, clamped short of the poles so the view never flips.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.orbit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pan!-Tuple{Camera, Real, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"pan!(cam, dx, dy, viewport_height)\n\nMove camera and pivot parallel to the image plane by dx, dy pixels, so the point under the cursor at the pivot depth follows the mouse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pan!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pause!-Tuple{BagPlayer}","page":"API","text":"pause!(player) -> player\n\nStop advancing; the scene keeps showing the current moment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pause!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pick!-Tuple{Viewer, Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"pick!(viewer, x, y; set_pivot=false) -> RayHit\n\nCast a ray through framebuffer pixel (x, y) against all meshes (BVH) and point clouds (chunk index, 4-pixel tolerance). Prints the hit in local and source coordinates and highlights picked points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pick!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pick_point-Tuple{PointCloud, PointIndex, Ray}","page":"API","text":"pick_point(pc, index, ray; angle=0.003) -> (t, point_index)\n\nClosest point (along the ray) lying within the cone of half-angle angle radians around ray, so the tolerance is a constant number of pixels at any distance; pass angle = pixels × fov / viewport_height. Only chunks whose boxes the cone can touch are scanned. (Inf32, 0) when nothing is found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pick_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.play!-Tuple{BagPlayer}","page":"API","text":"play!(player) -> player\n\nStart (or resume) playback; restarts from the beginning when at the end. Playback advances with tick!, which the viewer calls every frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.play!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.points_in_radius-Tuple{PointIndex, Any, Real}","page":"API","text":"points_in_radius(index, p, r) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices of all points within distance r of p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.points_in_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pose-Tuple{SpatialRender.Layer}","page":"API","text":"Current pose of a layer (layer → scene frame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.pose_matrix","page":"API","text":"pose_matrix(position, orientation=Quat(); scale=1) -> Mat4f\n\nHomogeneous transform T * R * S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pose_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.projection_matrix-Tuple{Camera}","page":"API","text":"Camera → clip matrix, OpenGL convention (NDC z ∈ [-1, 1]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.projection_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.push_scan!-Tuple{StreamingPointCloud, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"push_scan!(layer, points; pose=nothing, intensity=nothing, classification=nothing) -> layer\n\nAppend one scan. points is a Vector{PointVertex} or a vector of 3-vectors in the sensor frame; pose (a 4×4 matrix, or a (position, Quat) tuple) maps them into the layer frame, so each scan lands where the vehicle was when it was taken. Thread-safe.\n\nlive = add!(scene, StreamingPointCloud(2_000_000))\n@async for (pts, p, q) in lidar_feed\n    push_scan!(live, pts; pose = (p, q))\nend\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.push_scan!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.raycast-Tuple{AbstractVector, Ray}","page":"API","text":"raycast(items, ray; angle=0.003) -> RayHit\n\nClosest hit among (layer, accel) pairs (see SpatialRender.pick_layer); layer poses are applied, so the ray is given in the scene frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.raycast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.raycast-Tuple{BVH, Ray}","page":"API","text":"raycast(bvh, ray; tmax=Inf32) -> (t, triangle)\n\nClosest hit of ray (Möller–Trumbore), as distance along the ray and the 1-based triangle id; (Inf32, 0) on a miss. Allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.raycast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.read_las_header-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_las_header(path) -> LASHeader\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.read_las_header"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.render_frame!-Tuple{Renderer, Camera, Integer, Integer}","page":"API","text":"render_frame!(renderer, camera, width, height)\n\nDraw one frame into the currently bound framebuffer: opaque meshes, then point clouds, then blended lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.render_frame!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.render_offscreen-Tuple{Scene}","page":"API","text":"render_offscreen(scene; width=1024, height=768, camera=nothing, settings) -> Matrix{NTuple{3,UInt8}}\n\nRender one frame into a hidden framebuffer and return it as an image (rows top to bottom). With camera=nothing the camera frames the scene from the south-west, 30° above the horizon. Useful for CI snapshots and batch reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.render_offscreen"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.replay-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"replay(path; kwargs...) -> Viewer\n\nOpen a ROS 2 bag and replay it in a viewer window (playing from the start). Keyword arguments go to open_bag; title, width, height to the viewer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.replay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.run!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"API","text":"Run the event / render loop until the window is closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.run!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.save_las-Tuple{AbstractString, PointCloud}","page":"API","text":"save_las(path, pc; frame=IdentityFrame(), scale=0.001)\n\nWrite a LAS 1.2 file: point format 0, with GPS time 1, with colour 2, with both 3 (colour is written only when some point isn't plain white). Positions are mapped back to world coordinates through frame and quantised to scale metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.save_las"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.save_stl-Tuple{AbstractString, TriMesh}","page":"API","text":"save_stl(path, mesh)\n\nWrite a binary STL (used by tests and for exporting cleaned meshes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.save_stl"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.screen_ray-Tuple{Camera, Vararg{Real, 4}}","page":"API","text":"screen_ray(cam, x, y, width, height) -> Ray\n\nRay through pixel (x, y) (origin top-left, as GLFW reports the cursor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.screen_ray"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.screenshot-Tuple{AbstractString, Scene}","page":"API","text":"screenshot(path, scene; kwargs...) -> path\n\nrender_offscreen and save the image as PNG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.screenshot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.seek!-Tuple{BagPlayer, AbstractFloat}","page":"API","text":"seek!(player, t) -> player\n\nJump to log time t (nanoseconds; or a Float64 in seconds from the start). Short forward jumps continue playback; anything else rebuilds the streaming layers from the last history seconds before t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.seek!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.set_pivot!-Tuple{CameraController, Any}","page":"API","text":"Make p the orbit pivot without moving the camera (turns it to face p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.set_pivot!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.set_pose!","page":"API","text":"set_pose!(layer, position, orientation=Quat(); scale=1) -> layer\nset_pose!(layer, M::AbstractMatrix) -> layer\n\nPlace layer in the scene: its vertices are mapped by the pose into the scene frame. Thread-safe; takes effect on the next frame.\n\nrobot = add!(scene, load_stl(\"rover.stl\"))\nfor (p, yaw) in telemetry\n    set_pose!(robot, p, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.set_pose!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.slerp-Tuple{Quat, Quat, Real}","page":"API","text":"slerp(a, b, t) -> Quat\n\nSpherical linear interpolation between unit quaternions (shortest path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.slerp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.snapshot-Tuple{Scene}","page":"API","text":"snapshot(scene; width=1024, height=768, camera=nothing, settings) -> Snapshot\n\nrender_offscreen wrapped in a Snapshot, which notebooks and documentation show as an image.\n\nsnapshot(scene; width = 800, height = 450)   # in a notebook cell: the picture appears\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.snapshot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.step!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"API","text":"step!(viewer) -> Bool\n\nProcess events and draw one frame. Returns false once the window was closed (and releases it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.subset-Tuple{PointCloud, Any}","page":"API","text":"subset(pc, mask_or_indices) -> PointCloud\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.subset"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.tick!-Tuple{BagPlayer, Real}","page":"API","text":"tick!(player, dt) -> player\n\nAdvance playback by dt wall-clock seconds × rate (no-op while paused). Stops at the end, or wraps when player.loop is set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.tick!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.topics-Tuple{MCAPFile}","page":"API","text":"Topics with their schema names and message counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.topics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.touch!-Tuple{SpatialRender.Layer}","page":"API","text":"touch!(layer) -> layer\n\nMark the layer's contents (vertices, indices, polylines) as changed after editing them in place; the renderer re-uploads it on the next frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.touch!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.trajectory-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"trajectory(positions; frame=IdentityFrame(), times=nothing, colormap=:time,\n           color=Vec4f(0.2, 0.8, 1, 1), width=3, name=\"trajectory\") -> LineLayer\n\nTurn a sequence of positions (a vector of 3-vectors, or a 3×N / N×3 matrix) into a line layer. With colormap=:time (default) the path is shaded from blue (start) to red (end) along times (or the sample index), which makes loops and revisits easy to read. Use colormap=:solid for one color. max_gap (in the units of times) breaks the line wherever consecutive samples are further apart, so recording gaps don't show as straight jumps.\n\nPositions go through frame, so GNSS fixes can be given as (lon, lat, h) with an ENUFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.trajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.turbo-Tuple{Real}","page":"API","text":"turbo(x) -> Vec4f\n\nGoogle's Turbo colormap (polynomial approximation), x ∈ [0, 1]. The same polynomial is used in the point shader for height / intensity coloring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.turbo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.undo!-Tuple{Annotations}","page":"API","text":"Remove the most recent annotate! / measure! addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.undo!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SpatialRender.validate_shaders","page":"API","text":"validate_shaders(sources=BUILTIN_SHADERS; backend=:auto) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nCheck that every shader stage compiles. backend=:glslang runs glslangValidator (no GPU or display needed), backend=:gl compiles in a hidden GLFW window with the real driver, :auto prefers glslang. Returns one (name, stage, ok, log) record per stage; never throws on a shader error, so it suits CI reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.validate_shaders"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.view_matrix-Tuple{Camera}","page":"API","text":"World → camera matrix (Float32, column-major, ready for glUniformMatrix4fv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.view_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.with_update_lock-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"with_update_lock(f)\n\nRun f() holding the scene update lock: use it to change several layer fields at once (e.g. color and visibility) atomically with respect to the renderer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.with_update_lock"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.world_bounds-Tuple{SpatialRender.Layer}","page":"API","text":"Bounds of a layer in the scene frame (its pose applied).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.world_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.write_message!-Tuple{MCAPWriter, Integer, Integer, AbstractVector{UInt8}}","page":"API","text":"Append a message (log time in nanoseconds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.write_message!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SpatialRender.write_png-Tuple{IO, AbstractMatrix{Tuple{UInt8, UInt8, UInt8}}}","page":"API","text":"write_png(path_or_io, img::Matrix{NTuple{3,UInt8}})\n\nWrite an RGB image (rows top to bottom) as PNG to a file path (returns the path) or to any IO (returns the IO).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.write_png"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/octree/#Large-surveys-(out-of-core)","page":"Large surveys (out-of-core)","text":"build_octree(\"county.las\", \"county_octree\")    # once; streams the file, bounded RAM\nscene = Scene()\nload!(scene, \"county_octree\"; point_budget = 4_000_000)\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\nA 3M-point file converts in about 5 s. Coarse octree levels add about 10% storage over the raw points.","title":"Large surveys (out-of-core)"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/octree/#SpatialRender.build_octree-guide-octree","page":"Large surveys (out-of-core)","text":"build_octree(source, out_dir; node_capacity=50_000, bucket_points=4_000_000,\n             spacing_div=128, chunk=1_000_000, seed=1) -> out_dir\n\nConvert a point cloud for out-of-core rendering with OctreeCloud. source is a LAS file path or a PointCloud (whose coordinates are taken as already local).\n\nnode_capacity: maximum points stored in one node.\nbucket_points: target points per on-disk bucket. Peak memory is about bucket_points × 24 bytes plus write buffers.\nspacing_div: subsampling grid per node edge; larger keeps more points in coarse nodes.\n\nThe LAS path is read in chunk-point slices through a memory map, so files larger than RAM convert. Coordinates are stored relative to the cloud's centre (an OffsetFrame recorded in octree.json).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.build_octree"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/octree/#SpatialRender.OctreeCloud-guide-octree","page":"Large surveys (out-of-core)","text":"OctreeCloud(dir; point_budget=3_000_000, min_node_pixels=80, point_size=2, color_mode)\n\nA point cloud converted with build_octree and streamed from disk. With adaptive_size (default) each node's points are drawn at a world size matching that node's point spacing (never below point_size pixels), so coarse regions read as continuous surfaces rather than sparse dots.\n\nStreaming details: only the nodes that matter for the current view are loaded (on a background thread) and kept on the GPU, within point_budget points. Nodes are refined while their projected size exceeds min_node_pixels.\n\nThe layer's coordinates are relative to frame(cloud) (an OffsetFrame at the data centre); load other data with that frame to overlay it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.OctreeCloud"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Point-clouds","page":"Point clouds","text":"This tutorial follows a typical LiDAR workflow: load a LAS file, clean it, classify the ground, query it, and render it. At the end it shows how to view surveys too large for memory.\n\nTo keep the tutorial self-contained we first write a small synthetic survey (terrain, two buildings and some stray returns) as a LAS file, the way a real one arrives: in projected coordinates (here UTM-like eastings and northings).\n\nusing SpatialRender, Random\nrng = Xoshiro(7)\nterrain(x, y) = 0.4sin(x / 15) * cos(y / 20)\npts = Vec3f[]; cls = Int[]\nfor _ in 1:150_000                                     # ground\n    x, y = 120rand(rng) - 60, 120rand(rng) - 60\n    push!(pts, Vec3f(x, y, terrain(x, y) + 0.03randn(rng))); push!(cls, 1)\nend\nfor (cx, cy, h) in ((15, 10, 9), (-20, -15, 6)), _ in 1:20_000   # two buildings\n    x, y = cx + 14(rand(rng) - 0.5), cy + 14(rand(rng) - 0.5)\n    push!(pts, Vec3f(x, y, terrain(cx, cy) + (rand(rng) < 0.6 ? h : h * rand(rng)))); push!(cls, 1)\nend\nfor _ in 1:300                                          # stray returns (birds, dust)\n    push!(pts, Vec3f(120rand(rng) - 60, 120rand(rng) - 60, 15 + 25rand(rng))); push!(cls, 1)\nend\nsurvey = PointCloud(pts; intensity = rand(rng, length(pts)), classification = cls)\nutm = OffsetFrame(612_000.0, 5_123_000.0, 300.0)      # where the survey really is\nsave_las(\"survey.las\", survey; frame = utm)","title":"Point clouds"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Loading-a-LAS-file","page":"Point clouds","text":"load_las reads LAS 1.0–1.4 (point formats 0–10) through a memory map. It returns the cloud and the local frame it used: coordinates are stored relative to the file's centre, so they stay precise as 32-bit floats.\n\ncloud, frame = load_las(\"survey.las\")\nprintln(length(cloud), \" points; local origin \", frame.origin)\n190300 points; local origin [611999.9998130798, 5.122999998691559e6, 319.72614523768425]\n\nUseful options: stride = 10 keeps every tenth point (quick previews of huge files), classes = [2, 6] keeps only ground and buildings, and gps_time = true also reads per-point GPS time.","title":"Loading a LAS file"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Removing-stray-returns","page":"Point clouds","text":"Filters compute a keep-mask, so they never modify the original cloud and can be chained with |>. StatisticalOutlierFilter drops points whose nearest neighbours are unusually far away (the birds and dust):\n\nclean = StatisticalOutlierFilter(k = 8, nsigma = 2.5)(cloud)\nprintln(length(cloud) - length(clean), \" stray points removed\")\n300 stray points removed","title":"Removing stray returns"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Classifying-the-ground","page":"Point clouds","text":"GroundPlaneFilter fits the dominant near-horizontal plane with RANSAC. With mode = :classify it keeps every point and marks the ground as ASPRS class 2, so colouring by class separates ground from everything else. It assumes the ground is roughly one plane, which suits a site or a street; for hilly terrain, crop the area into tiles first.\n\nclassified = GroundPlaneFilter(threshold = 0.3, mode = :classify)(clean)\nclassified.color_mode = Int32(COLOR_CLASSIFICATION)\nscene = Scene(frame = frame)\nadd!(scene, classified)\ncamera = Camera(position = Vec3d(-70, -80, 55), target = Vec3d(0, 0, 0))\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nGround (brown) and everything else, coloured by class\n\nOther filters: VoxelDownsample (one point per voxel), RadiusOutlierFilter, and CropBox (e.g. to cut the vehicle itself out of a scan). A pipeline runs them in order:\n\npipeline = StatisticalOutlierFilter() |> VoxelDownsample(0.25)\nthin = pipeline(cloud)\nprintln(length(cloud), \" → \", length(thin), \" points\")\n190300 → 136489 points","title":"Classifying the ground"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Colouring","page":"Point clouds","text":"color_mode selects how points are coloured: COLOR_HEIGHT (the default), COLOR_INTENSITY, COLOR_CLASSIFICATION, COLOR_RGB (when the file has colour) or COLOR_AGE (for streamed scans). Eye-dome lighting, on by default, shades depth edges so shapes read well even without colour:\n\nclassified.color_mode = Int32(COLOR_HEIGHT)\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe same cloud coloured by height","title":"Colouring"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Queries","page":"Point clouds","text":"build_point_index sorts a cloud into spatial chunks (the renderer does this automatically when it first draws a cloud). The same index answers neighbour queries:\n\nindex = build_point_index(classified)\nroof = Vec3f(15, 10, terrain(15, 10) + 9)\nidx, dist = nearest_points(index, roof, 5)\nprintln(\"5 nearest points to the roof centre: \", round.(dist, digits = 2), \" m\")\nprintln(length(points_in_radius(index, roof, 2.0)), \" points within 2 m\")\n5 nearest points to the roof centre: Float32[19.73, 19.73, 19.73, 19.73, 19.73] m\n0 points within 2 m\n\nRays can be cast into the scene too, which is how the viewer picks points when you double-click: see raycast and pick_point.","title":"Queries"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/point_clouds/#Surveys-larger-than-memory","page":"Point clouds","text":"build_octree converts a LAS file into an out-of-core octree in a few streaming passes; its memory use is set by bucket_points, not by the file size. OctreeCloud (or load! on the directory) then streams only the parts the current view needs.\n\nA good habit is to clean once and convert the cleaned file, so stray returns don't end up in every view (or stretch the height colouring):\n\nsave_las(\"survey_clean.las\", clean; frame)\nbuild_octree(\"survey_clean.las\", \"survey_octree\"; node_capacity = 20_000)\nbig = Scene()\nload!(big, \"survey_octree\"; point_budget = 100_000)   # draw at most 100k points\noctree = big.layers[1]\nprintln(length(octree), \" points in \", length(octree.nodes), \" nodes\")\nsnapshot(big; width = 900, height = 500,\n           camera = Camera(position = Vec3d(-70, -80, 55), target = Vec3d(0, 0, 0)))\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe octree drawn within a 100k point budget\n\nEven with a tight budget the surface looks continuous: each node's points are drawn at a size that matches its point spacing.\n\nNext: Maps and coordinate frames.\n\n","title":"Surveys larger than memory"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/live/#Live-data-and-poses","page":"Live data and poses","text":"scene = Scene()\nrover = add!(scene, load_stl(\"rover.stl\"))\nlidar = add!(scene, StreamingPointCloud(2_000_000; max_age = 100))   # newest 2M points\nv = open_viewer(scene; block = false)\n\nThreads.@spawn for msg in sensor_feed           # any thread: updates are locked\n    set_pose!(rover, msg.position, msg.orientation)\n    push_scan!(lidar, msg.points; pose = (msg.sensor_position, msg.orientation))\nend\nrun!(v)\n\nexamples/live.jl simulates this with a ray-cast 16-beam LiDAR at 10 Hz. Use touch!(layer) after editing a layer's vertices in place.","title":"Live data and poses"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/live/#SpatialRender.set_pose!-guide-live","page":"Live data and poses","text":"set_pose!(layer, position, orientation=Quat(); scale=1) -> layer\nset_pose!(layer, M::AbstractMatrix) -> layer\n\nPlace layer in the scene: its vertices are mapped by the pose into the scene frame. Thread-safe; takes effect on the next frame.\n\nrobot = add!(scene, load_stl(\"rover.stl\"))\nfor (p, yaw) in telemetry\n    set_pose!(robot, p, Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), yaw))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.set_pose!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/live/#SpatialRender.pose_matrix-guide-live","page":"Live data and poses","text":"pose_matrix(position, orientation=Quat(); scale=1) -> Mat4f\n\nHomogeneous transform T * R * S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pose_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/live/#SpatialRender.touch!-guide-live","page":"Live data and poses","text":"touch!(layer) -> layer\n\nMark the layer's contents (vertices, indices, polylines) as changed after editing them in place; the renderer re-uploads it on the next frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.touch!"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/live/#SpatialRender.StreamingPointCloud-guide-live","page":"Live data and poses","text":"StreamingPointCloud(capacity; name=\"live\", point_size=2, color_mode=COLOR_AGE, max_age=50)\n\nA point layer for live sensor feeds holding the most recent capacity points. Fill it with push_scan!. COLOR_AGE shades points by how many scans ago they arrived (newest bright, max_age scans and older dark).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.StreamingPointCloud"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/live/#SpatialRender.push_scan!-guide-live","page":"Live data and poses","text":"push_scan!(layer, points; pose=nothing, intensity=nothing, classification=nothing) -> layer\n\nAppend one scan. points is a Vector{PointVertex} or a vector of 3-vectors in the sensor frame; pose (a 4×4 matrix, or a (position, Quat) tuple) maps them into the layer frame, so each scan lands where the vehicle was when it was taken. Thread-safe.\n\nlive = add!(scene, StreamingPointCloud(2_000_000))\n@async for (pts, p, q) in lidar_feed\n    push_scan!(live, pts; pose = (p, q))\nend\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.push_scan!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/viewer/#The-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"","title":"The viewer"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/viewer/#Controls","page":"The viewer","text":"Input Orbit mode Fly mode\nleft drag orbit about the pivot look\nright / middle / shift-left drag pan pan\nscroll zoom fly speed\nW A S D, Q E (shift = 4×) — move, down / up\ndouble-click pick: set the pivot and log the point in local + source coordinates same\n\nTab switches orbit ↔ fly · F frame all · T top view · O ortho · 1–5 color by height / intensity / class / RGB / scan age · +/- point size · P pixel ↔ world point size · L LOD on/off · M wireframe · G grid · B eye-dome lighting · R measure mode (click two points; Backspace undoes) · I status overlay · F12 screenshot · H help · Esc close.\n\nBag replay: Space play / pause · ← → seek 1 s (shift: 10 s) · [ ] half / double speed · Home restart.\n\nPass block = false to get the Viewer back and drive it yourself (step!(viewer)), for example from a robot's update loop. Layers added to the scene while it is open are uploaded on the next frame.","title":"Controls"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/viewer/#Labels-and-measurements","page":"The viewer","text":"Labels use a small stroke font drawn by the line shader, so no font files are needed. Measure mode in the viewer (R) calls measure! on the clicked points.","title":"Labels and measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/viewer/#Screenshots-and-headless-use","page":"The viewer","text":"snapshot, render_offscreen and screenshot render without opening a window; snapshot returns an image that notebooks display inline. On a machine without any display (a server or CI), GLFW falls back to its null platform and offscreen rendering still works where the OpenGL driver supports it. Set JULIA_GLFW_PLATFORM=null to say this is intended and silence GLFW's warnings.","title":"Screenshots and headless use"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.Annotations-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"Annotations(; name=\"annotations\", width=2)\n\nA layer of labels and measurement lines, drawn on top of the scene (never hidden by geometry). Add to it with annotate! and measure!; undo! removes the last addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Annotations"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.annotate!-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"annotate!(ann, position, text; color, size=13, marker=true) -> ann\n\nAdd a text label at position (layer frame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.annotate!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.measure!-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"measure!(ann, p, q; color=yellow) -> (distance, dz, slope_degrees)\n\nAdd a measurement from p to q: the segment, its 3-D length, the height difference and the slope, labelled at the midpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.measure!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.undo!-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"Remove the most recent annotate! / measure! addition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.undo!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.open_viewer-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"open_viewer(scene; width=1280, height=800, title, block=true, camera=nothing, settings,\n            vsync=true, player=nothing, visible=true) -> Viewer\n\nOpen a native window and render scene interactively (see HELP for controls; press H in the window). visible=false keeps the window hidden (automated tests, scripted captures). With block=false the function returns the Viewer immediately; call run!(viewer) or step!(viewer) to drive it, e.g. from a robot's own update loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_viewer"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.snapshot-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"snapshot(scene; width=1024, height=768, camera=nothing, settings) -> Snapshot\n\nrender_offscreen wrapped in a Snapshot, which notebooks and documentation show as an image.\n\nsnapshot(scene; width = 800, height = 450)   # in a notebook cell: the picture appears\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.render_offscreen-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"render_offscreen(scene; width=1024, height=768, camera=nothing, settings) -> Matrix{NTuple{3,UInt8}}\n\nRender one frame into a hidden framebuffer and return it as an image (rows top to bottom). With camera=nothing the camera frames the scene from the south-west, 30° above the horizon. Useful for CI snapshots and batch reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.render_offscreen"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.screenshot-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"screenshot(path, scene; kwargs...) -> path\n\nrender_offscreen and save the image as PNG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.screenshot"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.RenderSettings-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"RenderSettings(; kwargs...)\n\nbackground: clear color.\npoint_size_mode: :pixels (constant screen size) or :world (metres, shrinks with distance; world_point_size).\nround_points: circular splats with sphere shading (false: squares).\nlod_density: points drawn per pixel² of a chunk's screen footprint; Inf disables level of detail.\nwireframe_color: overlay color for meshes with wireframe = true.\ngrid: draw a ground grid at z = 0.\nedl, edl_strength, edl_radius: eye-dome lighting on points and meshes (screen-space depth shading; radius in pixels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.RenderSettings"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/viewer/#SpatialRender.pick!-guide-viewer","page":"The viewer","text":"pick!(viewer, x, y; set_pivot=false) -> RayHit\n\nCast a ray through framebuffer pixel (x, y) against all meshes (BVH) and point clouds (chunk index, 4-pixel tolerance). Prints the hit in local and source coordinates and highlights picked points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.pick!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#GPU,-memory-and-performance","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"","title":"GPU, memory and performance"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#GPU-on-WSL2","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"On WSL2, SpatialRender selects a hardware GPU before it opens its first window. Mesa reaches Windows GPUs through its d3d12 driver, but by default it often falls back to the CPU. On hybrid laptops it also picks the integrated GPU first, and if that GPU's WSL driver fails, Mesa gives up instead of trying the discrete one. SpatialRender sets GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12 and tries MESA_D3D12_DEFAULT_ADAPTER_NAME = NVIDIA, AMD, Intel, then the default. Each candidate is checked with a real context; if none works, the original environment is restored and rendering stays on the CPU. configure_gpu!() reports what was chosen.\n\nYour own GALLIUM_DRIVER is never overridden.\nSPATIALRENDER_GPU_AUTOCONFIG=0 disables the selection.\nTo give every OpenGL program in WSL the GPU, add these to ~/.profile (adjust the adapter name to your GPU):\nexport GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12\nexport MESA_D3D12_DEFAULT_ADAPTER_NAME=NVIDIA\n\nMeasured on a GTX 1650 laptop (WSLg, 1280×720, eye-dome lighting on):\n\nScene GPU (D3D12) CPU (llvmpipe)\n500k points, every point drawn 5.1 ms (195 fps) 58 ms\n5M points, every point drawn 14.5 ms (69 fps) 408 ms\n5M points, LOD 7.2 ms (139 fps) 79 ms\n\nAll vertex attributes are kept 4-byte aligned. D3D12-backed drivers otherwise convert vertex buffers on the CPU for every draw: an unaligned 1-byte attribute once made multi-range draws hang and lose the device. A test guards the layouts.","title":"GPU on WSL2"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#Memory","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"Measured on a 3M-point LAS file:\n\nStage Memory\npoint in RAM and on the GPU 20 bytes\nload_las (memory-mapped, threaded) 20 B/pt, the vertices only\nbuild_point_index 4 B/pt while building; about 16 KB retained in total\nstatistical / radius outlier filter ~53 / ~31 B/pt while running, nothing retained (1.7 s / 0.5 s for 3M points on 4 threads)\nvoxel_downsample ~18 B/pt while running\nbuild_octree about 60 MB of working memory for 3M points beyond the memory-mapped input: the bucket buffers plus one decode chunk, so it is set by bucket_points, not the file size\noctree at runtime the memory-mapped node file plus the GPU-resident nodes (at most 2 × the point budget)\n\nDependencies beyond Julia's standard library: GLFW and ModernGL (window and OpenGL), StaticArrays, JSON3 (GeoJSON and octree index), and the small Zstd_jll / Lz4_jll C libraries for compressed bags.","title":"Memory"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/performance/#Benchmark","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"benchmark/pipeline.jl measures the core pipeline on synthetic data (no GPU needed); CI runs it on every push and reports the table in the job summary:\n\njulia -t auto --project benchmark/pipeline.jl 2000000","title":"Benchmark"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/performance/#SpatialRender.configure_gpu!-guide-performance","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"configure_gpu!(; candidates=nothing, force=false) -> String\n\nMake OpenGL use a hardware GPU on WSL2 (see the comment at the top of src/render/gpu_select.jl). Called automatically before the first window; returns a description of what was selected.\n\nDoes nothing when not on WSL, when GALLIUM_DRIVER is already set (your choice wins), or when SPATIALRENDER_GPU_AUTOCONFIG=0. candidates are adapter-name substrings to try in order (default: an explicitly set MESA_D3D12_DEFAULT_ADAPTER_NAME, else NVIDIA, AMD, Intel, then Mesa's default adapter).\n\nprobe (returns the renderer string of a fresh context, or nothing), reinit and wsl exist so tests can simulate drivers; leave them alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.configure_gpu!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/performance/#SpatialRender.gl_info-guide-performance","page":"GPU, memory and performance","text":"gl_info() -> (vendor, renderer, version, software)\n\nDescribe the current GL context; software is true for CPU rasterisers (llvmpipe, softpipe, SwiftShader, Microsoft Basic Render).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.gl_info"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#Maps-and-coordinate-frames","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"Survey data, GIS layers and GNSS tracks come in global coordinates: longitude and latitude, or projected eastings and northings in the hundreds of thousands of metres. GPUs work in 32-bit floats, which at those magnitudes only resolve centimetres or worse. SpatialRender therefore moves everything into a local frame near the origin, in 64-bit precision, before anything reaches the GPU. This tutorial shows how to put different sources into the same frame.","title":"Maps and coordinate frames"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#The-two-kinds-of-frame","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"ENUFrame(lat, lon, h): a local East-North-Up tangent plane at a geodetic origin. Input coordinates are (lon, lat[, height]), the order GeoJSON uses.\nOffsetFrame(x, y, z): for data that is already projected (UTM, state plane, most LAS files); it subtracts the origin.\n\nusing SpatialRender\nenu = ENUFrame(47.3769, 8.5417)                      # Zürich main station\nto_local(enu, (8.5417, 47.3769))                    # the origin itself\n3-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 0.0\n 0.0\n 0.0\n\nto_local(enu, (8.5427, 47.3769))                    # 0.001° further east ≈ 75 m\n3-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 75.51950648804123\n  0.00048493109123704187\n -0.00044627795799469624\n\nto_world(enu, Vec3d(100, 50, 0))                    # back to (lon, lat, height)\n3-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n  8.543024172454446\n 47.377349720828114\n  0.0009787380695343018","title":"The two kinds of frame"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#GIS-layers","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"load_geojson turns lines and polygon rings into a line layer and points into markers. We write a small file with two building outlines and a route, as a GIS export would.\n\nlon(e) = 8.5417 + e / (111_320 * cosd(47.3769))     # metres east → longitude\nlat(n) = 47.3769 + n / 111_150                      # metres north → latitude\nring(e0, n0, w, h) = [[lon(e0), lat(n0)], [lon(e0 + w), lat(n0)], [lon(e0 + w), lat(n0 + h)],\n                      [lon(e0), lat(n0 + h)], [lon(e0), lat(n0)]]\nroute = [[lon(e), lat(0.002e^2 - 30)] for e in -60:5:60]\nwrite(\"site.geojson\", \"\"\"\n{\"type\": \"FeatureCollection\", \"features\": [\n  {\"type\": \"Feature\", \"properties\": {\"stroke\": \"#ffcc33\"},\n   \"geometry\": {\"type\": \"Polygon\", \"coordinates\": [$(ring(10, 5, 25, 15))]}},\n  {\"type\": \"Feature\", \"properties\": {\"stroke\": \"#ffcc33\"},\n   \"geometry\": {\"type\": \"Polygon\", \"coordinates\": [$(ring(-40, 10, 18, 30))]}},\n  {\"type\": \"Feature\", \"properties\": {\"stroke\": \"#33ddff\"},\n   \"geometry\": {\"type\": \"LineString\", \"coordinates\": $(route)}}\n]}\"\"\")\nlines, markers, frame = load_geojson(\"site.geojson\"; frame = enu, width = 3, height = 0.3)\nlength(lines.polylines)\n3\n\nheight = 0.3 lifts the 2-D outlines 30 cm above the ground, so they stay visible over the point cloud. When frame is not given, load_geojson creates an ENUFrame at the centre of the data. load! goes one step further: the first georeferenced file loaded into an empty scene sets the scene's frame, and every later file is placed in it.","title":"GIS layers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#Adding-a-GNSS-track","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"trajectory accepts (lon, lat, height) fixes when given an ENUFrame. Fixes recorded with gaps can be broken with max_gap (in the units of times) so the gap isn't drawn as a straight jump.\n\nfixes = [(lon(e), lat(0.002e^2 - 30 + 0.8sin(e)), 0.5) for e in -60:2.0:60]\ntimes = collect(1.0:length(fixes)); times[30:end] .+= 60     # a one-minute outage\ntrack = trajectory(fixes; frame = enu, times, max_gap = 5, width = 3, name = \"gnss\")\nlength(track.polylines)          # segments, without the jump across the outage\n59","title":"Adding a GNSS track"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#Putting-it-together","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"The outlines, the route and the GNSS track share the ENU frame, so they line up. A point cloud in the same frame (here a synthetic scan of the two buildings) completes the picture.\n\nusing Random\nrng = Xoshiro(3)\npts = Vec3f[]\nfor (e0, n0, w, h, height) in ((10, 5, 25, 15, 12), (-40, 10, 18, 30, 7)), _ in 1:15_000\n    x, y = e0 + w * rand(rng), n0 + h * rand(rng)\n    push!(pts, Vec3f(x, y, rand(rng) < 0.5 ? height : height * rand(rng)))\nend\nappend!(pts, [Vec3f(140rand(rng) - 70, 100rand(rng) - 50, 0.02randn(rng)) for _ in 1:60_000])\nscene = Scene(frame = enu)\nadd!(scene, PointCloud(pts; name = \"scan\"), lines, track)\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500,\n           camera = Camera(position = Vec3d(-60, -95, 70), target = Vec3d(0, 0, 0)))\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nBuilding outlines, route and GNSS track over a scan, all in one ENU frame","title":"Putting it together"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/maps_and_frames/#Projected-data","page":"Maps and coordinate frames","text":"LAS files and many GIS exports are in projected coordinates. Use an OffsetFrame for those, and pass the same frame to every loader:\n\npc, frame = load_las(\"survey.las\")                 # frame: OffsetFrame at the file centre\nlines, _, _ = load_geojson(\"parcels_wgs84.geojson\") # lon/lat: needs its own ENU frame\n\nThere is no map-projection library in SpatialRender, so lon/lat layers and projected layers can't be combined automatically. Either reproject the GIS layer to the survey's projection (and load it with the same OffsetFrame), or use the GeoInterface extension with GeoDataFrames, which can reproject while loading.\n\nNext: Live robot data.\n\n","title":"Projected data"},{"category":"section","location":"#SpatialRender.jl","page":"Home","text":"A native OpenGL renderer for meshes, LiDAR point clouds, GIS vector layers and trajectories in one scene, for diagnostic views in robotics and aerial systems. No browser, no plotting stack: GLFW + OpenGL 4.1 core, interleaved vertex buffers uploaded straight from Julia arrays, and a GPU-free core (ingestion, local frames, filters, BVH / k-d tree, culling, picking, camera) that runs and tests headless.\n\n(Image: demo)\n\n(Image: live)\n\nexamples/demo.jl (top): a cycloidal-drive disc (CAD), 480k LiDAR points after outlier removal, GIS parcels reprojected from lon/lat into ENU, and a UAV survey trajectory colored by time. examples/live.jl (bottom): a rover streaming a simulated LiDAR, points colored by scan age.\n\nNew here? Start with the tutorials: five short walk-throughs whose code runs on every documentation build.","title":"SpatialRender.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/rainerrodrigues/SpatialRender.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using SpatialRender\n\nscene = Scene()\nload!(scene, \"scan.las\")              # first georeferenced file sets the local frame\nload!(scene, \"boundaries.geojson\")    # reprojected into the same frame\nadd!(scene, load_stl(\"bracket.stl\"; scale = 1e-3))   # mm CAD → m\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/demo.jl            # interactive\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/demo.jl shot.png   # offscreen screenshot\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/live.jl            # rover + streaming LiDAR\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/octree.jl big.las  # out-of-core survey\njulia -t auto --project=. examples/replay.jl my_bag/  # replay a ROS 2 bag (no argument: demo bag)","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-in-the-box","page":"Home","text":"Stage Implementation\nIngestion Binary/ASCII STL, OBJ (native); LAS 1.0–1.4, point formats 0–10, via Mmap with threaded decoding (5M points ≈ 0.06 s); LAZ through the laszip/pdal CLI; GeoJSON; trajectories from positions or lon/lat/alt\nLocal frames ENUFrame(lat, lon, h) (WGS84 → ECEF → ENU in Float64), OffsetFrame for UTM/projected data. GPU data is Float32 offsets near the origin, so no precision loss\nPreprocessing Statistical and radius outlier removal, voxel downsampling, RANSAC ground plane (remove / keep / classify), crop box, composable with |>; AttributeOutlierFilter (z-score, IQR, MAD, Hampel) via the KaosCleaner engine\nIndexing Binned-SAH BVH for meshes (allocation-free ray casts). Point clouds are reordered in place into contiguous k-d tree chunks, which also answer k-NN and radius queries, so the index costs one entry per chunk rather than per point\nCulling + LOD Frustum culling of chunks into two pre-allocated Int32 arrays (first, count) that feed glMultiDrawArrays directly. Points are shuffled inside each chunk, so drawing a shorter prefix gives a uniform subsample. Culling 5M points takes ~8 µs per frame and allocates nothing\nGPU Interleaved PointVertex (20 B: position, RGBA, 16-bit intensity, class, flags; no padding), MeshVertex (32 B: position, normal, uv), per-instance LineVertex. These isbits layouts go to glBufferData without repacking, with every attribute 4-byte aligned\nShaders Points: pixel- or world-sized round splats colored by height / intensity / ASPRS class / RGB (Turbo map). Meshes: Blinn-Phong headlight, hemisphere ambient, two-sided, wireframe overlay. Lines: screen-space, anti-aliased, near-plane clipped instanced ribbons\nShader tooling Programs cached per context by source hash; validate_shaders() checks every stage with glslangValidator (CI, no GPU) or the live driver\nCamera Quaternion camera, turntable orbit (pitch-clamped), pan, zoom, fly mode, frame-all, top view, ortho toggle, pick-to-pivot\nPoses Every layer has a pose (set_pose!(layer, position, Quat) or a 4×4 matrix). Moving a layer is one uniform update; culling, LOD and picking run in the layer's own frame\nLive data StreamingPointCloud ring buffer: push_scan!(layer, points; pose) from any thread or task. Only dirty ranges are uploaded (glBufferSubData), nothing is re-indexed, and memory stays bounded. Optional coloring by scan age\nOut-of-core build_octree(las, dir) converts in streaming passes; peak RAM is one bucket (~4M points by default), not the survey. Nodes split only along long axes, so flat surveys become quadtrees. OctreeCloud streams nodes by screen size within a point budget, on a background thread, with LRU eviction. Point sizes adapt per node octant, so coarse areas still read as continuous surfaces\nBag replay Native MCAP / rosbag2 reader, ROS 2 message decoding, TF frame tree, timeline player with seek and speed control, status overlay\nEye-dome lighting Screen-space log-depth shading on points and meshes (B toggles it)\nAnnotations Labels (annotate!) and measurements (measure!: distance, Δz, slope) in an original stroke font, so no font files are needed; measure mode in the viewer\nOutput Interactive window; snapshot (shown inline in notebooks), render_offscreen / screenshot to PNG (zlib from Julia's own Zlib_jll) for CI and reports","title":"What is in the box"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tested-against-real-world-data","page":"Home","text":"Besides the unit tests, the readers were checked against public test data:\n\nData Source Result\n416 MCAP files covering every combination of chunking, indexes, padding, repeated records and summary sections Foxglove's MCAP conformance suite all decode to the expected messages, channels and schemas\nrosbag2 recordings, including split bags of 3 and 5 files the rosbag2 project's test resources message counts match each bag's metadata.yaml\n33 LAS files: versions 1.0–1.4, point formats 0–3, 6 and 7, extra bytes, many VLRs, deliberately broken files PDAL's test data identical to LasIO.jl on every point where LasIO can read the file (positions within 0.12 mm); the header bounds agree elsewhere\nASCII and binary STL (the same parts in both encodings), OBJ with normals and UVs three.js examples both encodings give identical triangles\n20 GeoJSON files with nulls, BOMs, collections and invalid JSON GDAL's test data geometry counts match an independent count\nA 25-minute, 1.3 GB recording from an autonomous shuttle bus: 9 LiDAR topics (Velodyne VLP-16, localisation and safety scanners), 4 odometry sources, GNSS and TF nUWAy2 (MIT) all 70 topic counts match metadata.yaml; every point cloud decodes in four real field layouts; scans placed via TF register scan to scan (no convention error: the inverse transform fits 9× worse)\n\nThe shuttle recording showed what real logs look like: /tf_static not recorded (sensor frames missing from TF), odometry in an unconnected odom frame, recording gaps of several minutes, heavily throttled LiDAR topics and data captured in bursts. The player handles each of these (see the replay section); the fixes are listed in the CHANGELOG.","title":"Tested against real-world data"},{"category":"section","location":"#Limits","page":"Home","text":"STEP / IGES are B-rep CAD, not meshes; tessellate them to STL/OBJ first (FreeCAD or OpenCASCADE). load_mesh says so instead of failing obscurely.\nLAZ needs laszip or pdal on PATH.\nProjected GIS layers need an OffsetFrame, or reprojection to EPSG:4326 for an ENUFrame. There is no PROJ dependency.\nBag replay decodes ROS 2 (CDR) messages only. ROS 1 messages inside MCAP are skipped, and rosbag2 SQLite (.db3) bags need ros2 bag convert to MCAP first. Big point clouds are decoded on the render thread, so very large scans at high rates can lower the frame rate during playback.\nThe GNSS overlay assumes the fixed frame's x/y axes point east/north.\nThe backend is OpenGL 4.1 core (the newest macOS supports), not WebGPU. Rendering is isolated in src/render/, so another backend can replace it without touching the core.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/replay_bags/#Replaying-ROS-2-bags","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"ROS 2 records robot data in bags; since ROS 2 Iron the default format is MCAP. SpatialRender reads them natively (no ROS installation needed), rebuilds the TF frame tree, and replays point clouds, odometry, paths and GNSS on a timeline.","title":"Replaying ROS 2 bags"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/replay_bags/#A-bag-to-work-with","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"So that this tutorial runs anywhere, we first record a short bag with MCAPWriter: a robot driving east at 1 m/s, a LiDAR seeing a wall, odometry and TF. A real bag from ros2 bag record works exactly the same way.\n\nusing SpatialRender\nsec = 1_000_000_000\nw = MCAPWriter(\"drive.mcap\")\nschema(name) = add_schema!(w, name)\ntf    = add_channel!(w, \"/tf\", schema(\"tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage\"))\nodom  = add_channel!(w, \"/odom\", schema(\"nav_msgs/msg/Odometry\"))\ncloud = add_channel!(w, \"/lidar/points\", schema(\"sensor_msgs/msg/PointCloud2\"))\nusing Random\nrng = Xoshiro(2)\nwall = [Vec3f(20, y, z) for y in -10:0.25:10 for z in 0:0.25:3]           # a wall 20 m east\nfor k in 0:100                                                            # 10 s at 10 Hz\n    t = k * sec ÷ 10; x = k / 10\n    write_message!(w, tf, t, encode_tf([\n        StampedTransform(t, \"map\", \"base_link\", Vec3d(x, 0, 0), Quat()),\n        StampedTransform(t, \"base_link\", \"lidar\", Vec3d(0.5, 0, 1.5), Quat())]))\n    write_message!(w, odom, t, encode_odometry(StampedTransform(t, \"map\", \"base_link\", Vec3d(x, 0, 0), Quat())))\n    # the scan, in the lidar's own frame: the wall as seen from the robot\n    seen = [p - Vec3f(x + 0.5, 0, 1.5) + 0.02f0 * Vec3f(randn(rng, 3)...) for p in wall]   # with sensor noise\n    write_message!(w, cloud, t, encode_pointcloud2(t, \"lidar\", seen))\nend\nclose(w)","title":"A bag to work with"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/replay_bags/#Opening-a-bag","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"open_bag indexes the file (or a rosbag2 directory, including recordings split over several files) and adds a layer for each supported topic.\n\nplayer = open_bag(\"drive.mcap\")\nplayer.bag                  # topics, types and message counts\nMCAPFile drive.mcap: 303 messages, 10.00 s, 2 chunks\n  /lidar/points  sensor_msgs/msg/PointCloud2  101\n  /odom          nav_msgs/msg/Odometry        101\n  /tf            tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage       101\n\n\nplayer.scene\nScene with 3 layers (frame: IdentityFrame)\n  StreamingPointCloud  /lidar/points                      1053 of 2000000 points, streaming\n  LineLayer            /odom trail                        100 polylines\n  LineLayer            /odom pose                         3 polylines\n\n\nSupported topics:\n\nMessage type Shown as\nsensor_msgs/PointCloud2 streaming cloud; each scan is placed through TF at its own timestamp\nnav_msgs/Odometry, geometry_msgs/PoseStamped trajectory plus an axes marker at the current pose\nnav_msgs/Path the latest path at the current time\nsensor_msgs/NavSatFix GNSS track\ntf2_msgs/TFMessage the frame tree","title":"Opening a bag"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/replay_bags/#Moving-through-time","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"seek! jumps to a time (seconds from the start); play! and tick! advance it. The scene always shows the moment the player is at.\n\nseek!(player, 7.0)\ncamera = Camera(position = Vec3d(-6, -14, 10), target = Vec3d(10, 0, 1))\nsnapshot(player.scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe robot at t = 7 s, with the last few scans of the wall\n\nThe wall is in the same place in every scan although the robot moved: each scan was transformed with the robot's pose at its timestamp.\n\nIn a window, replay does everything at once:\n\nreplay(\"drive.mcap\")        # Space: play/pause, ← →: seek, [ ]: speed, Home: restart","title":"Moving through time"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/replay_bags/#Real-recordings","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"Real bags are rarely complete, and the player is built for the common problems:\n\nSensor frames missing from TF. Static transforms (the sensor mountings on /tf_static) are often published once, before recording starts, so the bag doesn't contain them. The player then draws those sensors at the robot's base frame, with a warning, instead of hundreds of metres away. Supply the real mountings when you know them:\n\nmount = StampedTransform(0, \"base_link\", \"lidar\", Vec3d(0.5, 0, 1.5), Quat())\nplayer2 = open_bag(\"drive.mcap\"; static_transforms = [mount])\n\nRecording gaps. Trails are broken at gaps instead of jumping across them, and the robot's pose is not interpolated across gaps longer than max_tf_gap seconds.\nThrottled sensors. With history = :auto (the default) enough scans stay visible for the sparsest LiDAR topic.\nData recorded in bursts. Playback skips stretches with no messages (skip_gaps = true).\n\nTo look at the data itself, the lower-level pieces are available: open_mcap gives the message index, message_data a message's bytes, and decode_pointcloud2, decode_odometry, decode_tf and friends turn them into Julia values.\n\nbag = open_mcap(\"drive.mcap\")\nm = first(x for x in bag.messages if bag.channels[x.channel].topic == \"/lidar/points\")\nstamp, frame_id, points = decode_pointcloud2(message_data(bag, m))\nprintln(length(points), \" points in frame \\\"\", frame_id, \"\\\"\")\n1053 points in frame \"lidar\"\n\n","title":"Real recordings"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/loading/#Loading-data-and-local-frames","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"Every loader puts its data into a local Cartesian frame (metres, Z up) near the origin, so that Float32 GPU coordinates keep millimetre precision. load! picks the frame from the first georeferenced file and reuses it for the rest.","title":"Loading data and local frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/loading/#Frames","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"","title":"Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/loading/#Point-cloud-cleanup","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"pc, frame = load_las(\"flight.las\"; gps_time = true)\nclean = pc |> (GroundPlaneFilter(mode = :classify) |>\n               StatisticalOutlierFilter(k = 8, nsigma = 2.0) |>\n               VoxelDownsample(0.05))\n\nusing KaosCleaner                                      # enables the extension\nclean = AttributeOutlierFilter(attributes = [:intensity], method = \"hampel\")(clean)","title":"Point cloud cleanup"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/loading/#Package-extensions","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"Load Enables\nKaosCleaner AttributeOutlierFilter (per-attribute 1-D outlier statistics; Hampel windows follow GPS time)\nGeometryBasics + FileIO (+ MeshIO) TriMesh(::GeometryBasics.Mesh); load_mesh falls back to FileIO for PLY, OFF, …\nGeoInterface (e.g. via GeoDataFrames) gis_layer(df.geometry; frame = scene.frame) for shapefiles, GeoPackage, any OGR source\n\nGeometryBasics also exports Vec3f; qualify it (SpatialRender.Vec3f) when using both.","title":"Package extensions"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load!-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load!(scene, path; kwargs...) -> scene\n\nLoad any supported file into scene in the scene's frame.\n\nWhen the scene is still empty and uses an IdentityFrame, georeferenced data sets the frame: a LAS file sets an OffsetFrame at its centre, a GeoJSON file an ENUFrame at its centre. Later files reuse that frame. A directory written by build_octree loads as an OctreeCloud.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_mesh-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_mesh(path; kwargs...) -> TriMesh\n\nLoad a triangle mesh by extension: .stl and .obj natively. Other mesh formats (PLY, OFF, 3MF, …) load through FileIO/MeshIO once GeometryBasics is imported (package extension).\n\nSTEP / IGES are B-rep CAD formats, not meshes: tessellate them first (for example FreeCAD or OpenCASCADE STEP → STL export with a chosen tolerance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_mesh"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_stl-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_stl(path; smooth=false, frame=IdentityFrame(), name=basename(path), scale=1) -> TriMesh\n\nRead a binary or ASCII STL file.\n\nWith smooth=false (the default, right for CAD parts) every facet keeps its own three vertices and the facet normal, so hard edges stay hard. With smooth=true coincident vertices are welded and normals averaged. scale converts units (e.g. scale=1e-3 for millimetre CAD into metres). up=:y converts a Y-up model (common from 3D modelling tools) to the scene's Z-up convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_stl"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_obj-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_obj(path; frame=IdentityFrame(), scale=1, name=basename(path)) -> TriMesh\n\nRead a Wavefront OBJ. Polygons are fan-triangulated; each distinct v/vt/vn combination becomes one GPU vertex. Missing normals are computed. OBJ files from modelling tools are usually Y-up: pass up=:y to convert them to the scene's Z-up convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_obj"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_las-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_las(path; frame=nothing, name=basename(path), gps_time=false, stride=1,\n         classes=nothing) -> (PointCloud, frame)\n\nRead a LAS file through a memory map.\n\nframe: local frame for the points. When nothing, an OffsetFrame at the centre of the file's bounding box is used. The frame is returned too, so other layers can be loaded into the same coordinates.\nstride: keep every stride-th point (quick previews of huge files).\nclasses: keep only these ASPRS classes, e.g. classes=[2, 6].\ngps_time: also read per-point GPS time (formats 1, 3–10).\n\nIntensity is normalised to 0–1 by the largest value in the file; 16-bit RGB is reduced to 8 bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_las"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_laz-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_laz(path; kwargs...) -> (PointCloud, frame)\n\nDecompress a LAZ file with laszip or pdal (whichever is on PATH) into a temporary LAS file and read it with load_las.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_laz"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.save_las-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"save_las(path, pc; frame=IdentityFrame(), scale=0.001)\n\nWrite a LAS 1.2 file: point format 0, with GPS time 1, with colour 2, with both 3 (colour is written only when some point isn't plain white). Positions are mapped back to world coordinates through frame and quantised to scale metres.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.save_las"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.load_geojson-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"load_geojson(path; frame=nothing, color, width=2, height=0, name) -> (LineLayer, PointCloud, frame)\n\nRead a GeoJSON file. Lines and polygon rings become a LineLayer; Point/MultiPoint features become a PointCloud of markers.\n\nCoordinates are taken as WGS84 longitude/latitude and reprojected into frame. With frame=nothing an ENUFrame at the centre of the data's lon/lat bounds is used. height is added to 2-D coordinates (drape boundaries slightly above terrain). A feature property \"stroke\" holding a #rrggbb color overrides color.\n\nFor shapefiles, GeoPackage and other OGR formats load GeoDataFrames and use gis_layer (a package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.load_geojson"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.trajectory-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"trajectory(positions; frame=IdentityFrame(), times=nothing, colormap=:time,\n           color=Vec4f(0.2, 0.8, 1, 1), width=3, name=\"trajectory\") -> LineLayer\n\nTurn a sequence of positions (a vector of 3-vectors, or a 3×N / N×3 matrix) into a line layer. With colormap=:time (default) the path is shaded from blue (start) to red (end) along times (or the sample index), which makes loops and revisits easy to read. Use colormap=:solid for one color. max_gap (in the units of times) breaks the line wherever consecutive samples are further apart, so recording gaps don't show as straight jumps.\n\nPositions go through frame, so GNSS fixes can be given as (lon, lat, h) with an ENUFrame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.trajectory"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.Scene-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"Scene(; frame=IdentityFrame())\n\nA collection of layers sharing one local frame. Loaders accept frame= so data from different sources lands in the same coordinates; scene.frame is the natural value to pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.Scene"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.add!-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"add!(scene, layer) -> layer\n\nAdd a layer to the scene. Returns the layer so it can be tweaked afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.add!"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.ENUFrame-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"ENUFrame(lat0, lon0, h0=0)\n\nLocal East-North-Up tangent frame at a geodetic origin (degrees, metres). Source points are (lon, lat[, h]), the GeoJSON axis order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ENUFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.OffsetFrame-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"OffsetFrame(origin)\n\nFor projected coordinates (UTM, state plane, LAS files): subtract origin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.OffsetFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.IdentityFrame-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"IdentityFrame()\n\nCoordinates are already local (CAD parts, simulated data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.IdentityFrame"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.geodetic_to_ecef-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"geodetic_to_ecef(lat, lon, h) -> Vec3d\n\nWGS84 latitude / longitude in degrees and ellipsoidal height in metres to Earth-centred, Earth-fixed coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.geodetic_to_ecef"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.ecef_to_geodetic-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"ecef_to_geodetic(p) -> (lat, lon, h)\n\nInverse of geodetic_to_ecef (Bowring's method with two refinement iterations; sub-millimetre near the Earth's surface).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.ecef_to_geodetic"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.StatisticalOutlierFilter-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"StatisticalOutlierFilter(; k=8, nsigma=2.0)\n\nStatistical outlier removal (as in PCL's StatisticalOutlierRemoval): for each point, the mean distance to its k nearest neighbours; points whose mean distance exceeds the global mean by more than nsigma standard deviations are dropped. Removes isolated returns (dust, rain, multipath).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.StatisticalOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.RadiusOutlierFilter-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"RadiusOutlierFilter(; radius, min_neighbors=4)\n\nDrop points with fewer than min_neighbors other points within radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.RadiusOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.VoxelDownsample-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"VoxelDownsample(voxel)\n\nKeep one point per voxel-sized cube (the point nearest the voxel centre), so attributes stay real sensor values rather than averages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.VoxelDownsample"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.GroundPlaneFilter-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"GroundPlaneFilter(; threshold=0.15, iterations=200, max_tilt=15, mode=:remove, seed=1)\n\nFit the dominant near-horizontal plane with RANSAC (normal within max_tilt degrees of +Z) and treat points within threshold metres of it as ground.\n\nmode=:remove drops ground points (obstacle / structure views);\nmode=:keep keeps only ground points;\nmode=:classify keeps everything and sets ASPRS class 2 (ground) on the inliers, so the classification color mode shows them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.GroundPlaneFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.CropBox-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"CropBox(lo, hi; invert=false)\n\nKeep points inside (or with invert=true, outside) an axis-aligned box in the local frame, e.g. to cut the ego-vehicle out of a scan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.CropBox"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.AttributeOutlierFilter-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"AttributeOutlierFilter(; attributes=(:z, :intensity), method=\"mad\", threshold=nothing, window=7)\n\nOne-dimensional outlier rejection on per-point attributes (:x, :y, :z, :intensity); a point is dropped when any listed attribute is an outlier. Methods: \"zscore\", \"iqr\", \"mad\" (default, robust) and \"hampel\" (a moving-window MAD along acquisition order, GPS time when present, which follows a drifting signal: intensity spikes along a scan line).\n\nThe statistics are computed by the KaosCleaner engine: using KaosCleaner enables this filter (package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.AttributeOutlierFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.FilterPipeline-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"FilterPipeline(filters...)\n\nRun filters in order; each sees the output of the previous one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.FilterPipeline"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/loading/#SpatialRender.apply_filter-guide-loading","page":"Loading data and local frames","text":"apply_filter(f, pc) -> PointCloud\n\nA new cloud holding the points f keeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.apply_filter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This tutorial builds a small scene with the three basic kinds of layer (a mesh, a point cloud and a line) and renders it to an image. Every code block on this page runs when the documentation is built; the images are its output.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/rainerrodrigues/SpatialRender.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#A-scene","page":"Getting started","text":"A Scene holds layers that share one coordinate frame. Coordinates are metres, with Z up.\n\nusing SpatialRender\nscene = Scene()","title":"A scene"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#A-mesh","page":"Getting started","text":"Meshes are usually loaded from STL or OBJ files with load_mesh. Here we build a small box directly so the tutorial needs no files. A TriMesh is a vertex list plus triangle indices (three 0-based indices per triangle).\n\ncorners = [Vec3f(x, y, z) for z in (0, 1) for y in (-1, 1) for x in (-2, 2)]\nfaces = [(1, 2, 4), (1, 4, 3), (5, 7, 8), (5, 8, 6), (1, 5, 6), (1, 6, 2),\n         (3, 4, 8), (3, 8, 7), (1, 3, 7), (1, 7, 5), (2, 6, 8), (2, 8, 4)]\nvertices = MeshVertex[]\nindices = UInt32[]\nfor f in faces, k in f\n    push!(vertices, MeshVertex(corners[k], Vec3f(0, 0, 1)))\n    push!(indices, length(vertices) - 1)\nend\nbox = TriMesh(vertices, indices; name = \"box\", color = Vec4f(0.8, 0.55, 0.3, 1))\ncompute_normals!(box)     # proper normals for lighting\nadd!(scene, box)\nTriMesh \"box\" (12 triangles)","title":"A mesh"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#A-point-cloud","page":"Getting started","text":"A PointCloud can be made from a vector of points, with optional intensity, colour and classification per point. Real data usually comes from LAS files (see the point cloud tutorial).\n\nusing Random\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nground = [Vec3f(16rand(rng) - 8, 16rand(rng) - 8, 0.05randn(rng)) for _ in 1:60_000]\nadd!(scene, PointCloud(ground; intensity = rand(rng, length(ground)), name = \"ground\"))\nPointCloud \"ground\" (60000 points)","title":"A point cloud"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#A-trajectory","page":"Getting started","text":"trajectory turns a sequence of positions into a line layer, coloured from blue (start) to red (end) so the direction of travel is visible.\n\npath = [Vec3d(6cos(t), 6sin(t), 0.5 + 0.1t) for t in range(0, 4π, length = 200)]\nadd!(scene, trajectory(path; width = 4, name = \"path\"))\nscene\nScene with 3 layers (frame: IdentityFrame)\n  TriMesh              box                                12 triangles\n  PointCloud           ground                             60000 points\n  LineLayer            path                               199 polylines\n","title":"A trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Rendering-an-image","page":"Getting started","text":"snapshot renders without opening a window and returns an image, which notebooks (Jupyter, VS Code, Pluto) and these pages display directly. With no camera given, it frames the whole scene from the south-west.\n\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe first scene\n\nTo save a file instead, use screenshot(\"scene.png\", scene), or write_png(\"scene.png\", snapshot(scene).pixels).\n\nFor a chosen viewpoint, pass a Camera:\n\ncamera = Camera(position = Vec3d(10, -14, 9), target = Vec3d(0, 0, 0.5))\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe same scene from another viewpoint","title":"Rendering an image"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#The-interactive-viewer","page":"Getting started","text":"On a machine with a display, open_viewer shows the scene in a window:\n\nopen_viewer(scene)\n\nDrag with the left mouse button to orbit, the right button to pan, and scroll to zoom; press H in the window for all controls. Useful keys to try first: 1–5 change the point colouring, M toggles the mesh wireframe, R enters measure mode (click two points), and F frames the whole scene.","title":"The interactive viewer"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/getting_started/#Changing-the-scene","page":"Getting started","text":"Layers can be changed after they are added. Moving a layer is cheap: it only changes its pose, not its data.\n\nset_pose!(box, Vec3d(0, 0, 2), Quat(Vec3d(0, 0, 1), π / 6))   # lift and turn the box\nsnapshot(scene; width = 900, height = 500, camera)\nSnapshot(900×500)\n\nThe box moved by its pose\n\nAfter editing a layer's vertices in place, call touch! so the next frame uploads the new data.\n\nNext: Point clouds.","title":"Changing the scene"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/replay/#Replaying-ROS-2-bags","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"replay(\"flight_2026_11_20/\")          # rosbag2 directory or .mcap file; opens a viewer and plays\n\nOr, step by step:\n\np = open_bag(\"drive.mcap\"; history = 4.0)   # seconds of scans kept on screen\np.bag                                       # topics, types, message counts\nseek!(p, 12.5); play!(p)\nopen_viewer(p.scene; player = p)\n\n(Image: replay)\n\nexamples/replay.jl: a recorded rover bag at t = 12 s. LiDAR scans are placed through the TF chain map → odom → base_link → lidar at their own timestamps. The odometry trail is colored by time, the GNSS fixes (pink) are anchored to it, and the green line is the current plan.\n\nA native MCAP reader handles uncompressed, zstd and lz4 chunks and needs no ROS installation. Each topic type is drawn like this:\n\nTopic type Drawn as\ntf2_msgs/TFMessage (/tf, /tf_static) the frame tree, interpolated (slerp) at each message's stamp\nsensor_msgs/PointCloud2 streaming cloud colored by scan age. Reads float32/float64 xyz, any-type intensity, packed RGB; ring becomes the class\nnav_msgs/Odometry, geometry_msgs/PoseStamped trajectory colored by time, plus an axes marker at the interpolated current pose\nnav_msgs/Path the latest plan at the current time\nsensor_msgs/NavSatFix ENU track whose first fix is anchored at the robot's pose\n\nThe fixed frame is map, world or odom (whichever exists), else the root of the TF tree; fixed_frame = overrides it.\n\nReal recordings are often incomplete, and the player is built for that:\n\nSensor frames missing from TF (typically when /tf_static was published before recording started) are drawn at the robot's base frame, with a warning. Give the real mountings with open_bag(bag; static_transforms = [StampedTransform(0, \"base_link\", \"lidar_front\", Vec3d(1.2, 0, 1.8), Quat())]), or choose missing_frames = :origin / :skip.\nRecording gaps break trails instead of drawing straight jumps, and TF is only interpolated across gaps up to max_tf_gap seconds (default 2).\nSparse or throttled LiDAR topics: history = :auto keeps enough scans on screen for the sparsest one.\nData recorded in bursts: with skip_gaps = true (default) playback jumps over stretches with no messages. MCAPWriter and the\n\nencode_* functions write bags from Julia: examples/replay.jl records its demo bag this way.","title":"Replaying ROS 2 bags"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.replay-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"replay(path; kwargs...) -> Viewer\n\nOpen a ROS 2 bag and replay it in a viewer window (playing from the start). Keyword arguments go to open_bag; title, width, height to the viewer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.replay"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.open_bag-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"open_bag(path; scene=Scene(), fixed_frame=nothing, topics=nothing, cloud_capacity=2_000_000,\n         history=3.0, intensity_range=nothing, marker_size=nothing) -> BagPlayer\n\nOpen a ROS 2 bag (an .mcap file or a rosbag2 directory) for replay into scene and add a layer per supported topic:\n\nmessage type shown as\nsensor_msgs/PointCloud2 streaming cloud, each scan placed via TF at its stamp\nnav_msgs/Odometry, PoseStamped full trajectory + an axes marker at the current pose\nnav_msgs/Path the latest path at the current time\nsensor_msgs/NavSatFix ENU track, first fix anchored at the robot's pose\n\n/tf and /tf_static build the frame tree. Everything is drawn in fixed_frame (default: map, world, odom, else the tree's root). history seconds of scans stay visible (:auto: enough for the sparsest point cloud topic, 3–30 s). Sensor frames missing from TF (typically when /tf_static was not recorded) are drawn at the robot frame with missing_frames = :robot (or untransformed with :origin, or not at all with :skip); give the real mountings with static_transforms = [StampedTransform(...)]. TF is interpolated only across gaps up to max_tf_gap seconds. With skip_gaps, playback jumps over stretches with no messages. topics restricts which topics are shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_bag"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.seek!-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"seek!(player, t) -> player\n\nJump to log time t (nanoseconds; or a Float64 in seconds from the start). Short forward jumps continue playback; anything else rebuilds the streaming layers from the last history seconds before t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.seek!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.tick!-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"tick!(player, dt) -> player\n\nAdvance playback by dt wall-clock seconds × rate (no-op while paused). Stops at the end, or wraps when player.loop is set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.tick!"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.open_mcap-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"open_mcap(path) -> MCAPFile\n\nOpen an .mcap file, or a rosbag2 directory. A directory may hold a split recording (several .mcap files, as ros2 bag record --max-bag-size writes); all of them are read, in the order metadata.yaml lists them (else by name), into one time-sorted index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.open_mcap"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.MCAPWriter-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"MCAPWriter(path; profile=\"ros2\", compression=\"zstd\", chunk_size=1 << 20)\n\nWrite an MCAP file: add_schema!, add_channel!, write_message!, then close. Messages are grouped into chunks compressed with compression (\"zstd\", \"lz4\" or \"\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.MCAPWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.FrameTree-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"FrameTree(; max_gap=2.0)\n\nFrames and their transforms over time, filled from /tf and /tf_static (see add_transform!) and queried with lookup_transform. Between two samples more than max_gap seconds apart (a recording gap), the nearest sample is used instead of interpolating motion that wasn't recorded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.FrameTree"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.lookup_transform-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"lookup_transform(tree, target, source, t) -> Union{Rigid, Nothing}\n\nTransform taking points in frame source to frame target at time t (nanoseconds), or nothing when the frames are not connected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.lookup_transform"},{"category":"function","location":"guide/replay/#SpatialRender.decode_pointcloud2-guide-replay","page":"Replaying ROS 2 bags","text":"decode_pointcloud2(d; intensity_range=nothing) -> (stamp, frame_id, Vector{PointVertex})\n\nDecode a sensormsgs/PointCloud2. Uses the x, y, z fields (float32 or float64), intensity (any numeric type; normalised by `intensityrange, default 255 for floats and the type's maximum for integers),rgb/rgba(packed) andring` (stored as the classification, so class colouring shows LiDAR rings). Non-finite points are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SpatialRender.decode_pointcloud2"}]
}
